.\" Manual page for tacctl, the management CLI for pluggable network-device AAA.
.\" Render locally with: groff -man -Tascii man/tacctl.1 | less
.TH TACCTL 1 "2026-10-16" "tacctl 0.2.13" "tacctl Manual"
.SH NAME
tacctl \- manage pluggable network-device AAA: users, groups, scopes and devices
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tacctl
.I command
.RI [ subcommand ]
.RI [ arguments... ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tacctl
is a management CLI for network-device AAA. It keeps users,
groups, scopes (named CIDR + shared-secret bundles) and connection filters
in a store of its own,
.IR /etc/tacctl/store.yaml ,
and renders the configuration of every enabled
.I backend
from it: TACACS+ (RFC 8907) served by
.BR tacquito ,
installed by
.BR "tacctl install" ,
and, opt-in, RADIUS served by a tacctl-owned FreeRADIUS instance
.RB ( "tacctl backend enable radius" ).
It installs and upgrades the servers, inspects logs and backups, enrolls
Linux hosts (pam_tacplus or pam_radius_auth), and generates ready-to-paste
device configuration for Cisco IOS/IOS-XE and Juniper Junos, plus a
menu-by-menu setup procedure for WTI console servers, for either protocol.
See
.BR BACKENDS ,
.B "STORE AND GENERATED CONFIGURATION"
and
.BR UPGRADING .
.PP
Most commands require root.
.B tacctl
re-executes itself under
.BR sudo (8)
automatically when invoked by a non-root user.
.BR "tacctl hash" ,
.BR "tacctl version" ,
.B tacctl completion
and
.B tacctl shell
are deliberate exceptions: they run as the invoking user.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS System lifecycle
.TP
.BI "install [--branch " name "] [-y|--yes]"
Install tacctl and the TACACS+ backend. Builds the tacquito binary with the
Go toolchain (installed by
.I /opt/tacctl/bin/tacctl.sh
when missing), installs
.I /usr/local/bin/tacctl
(the verified release binary when the clone in
.I /opt/tacctl
is at a release tag, otherwise built from the clone),
installs the shell completion and this manual, creates the
.B tacquito
service user and the state directory
.IR /etc/tacctl ,
seeds the store (built-in groups and users, the scope
.B lab
with RFC 1918 prefixes and a generated shared secret, printed once),
renders
.I /etc/tacquito/tacquito.yaml
from it, installs the systemd units, and enables the service. An existing
store, or an existing
.IR tacquito.yaml ,
is kept (the latter goes through the upgrade's store gate). RADIUS
is not installed: see
.BR "backend enable" .
The plan is shown and confirmed
.RB ( [y/N] ).
.IP
Four built-in users are seeded:
.B engineer
.RB ( superuser ),
.B operator
.RB ( operator ),
.B viewer
.RB ( readonly ),
and
.B root
.RB ( readonly ).
.IP
The first three carry a placeholder hash that never authenticates;
activate one with
.B "tacctl user passwd " <name>
(a real password enables the account).
.B root
is an accounting-only sink: its hash stays disabled, and
.B "tacctl user passwd root"
is rejected. Without the built\-in
.B root
user, tacquito logs an error for every accounting packet of the Junos internal
daemons. Records of
.B root
with no terminal (port
.BR non\-tty ,
.B unknown
or empty), such as the Junos internal daemons' sessions, are answered with
success and not recorded. A
.B root
login on the console or over ssh has a port and is recorded, its commands
included.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command install
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-branch\fR \fI<name>\fR
The tacctl repo branch to install or upgrade from (default: the current one).
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Answer yes to the confirmations.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command install
.TP
.BI "upgrade [--branch " name "]"
Move tacctl state into
.I /etc/tacctl
(idempotent), pull and rebuild tacquito when upstream or the patch overlay
changed, pull the tacctl repository in
.I /opt/tacctl
(after switching to
.BI --branch " name"
when given) and, when the installed binary was not built from the commit
now checked out, replace it (the verified release binary at a release tag,
otherwise built from the clone) and re-execute it once, install
packages the new release needs, bring every enabled
backend's configuration in line with the release (a re-render from the
store; RADIUS restarts when its files or unit drop-in changed), refresh
system files (a device template you changed is kept; see
.I /etc/tacctl/templates/
under
.BR FILES ),
move an install that has no store yet into the store behind a
gate, and restart tacquito once if its binary, a unit or drop-in, or its
configuration changed (rolling the binary and unit files back if it does not
come up). A new README, logrotate file, completion or
template restarts nothing, and neither does an upgrade with nothing new.
Listener, log-level and metrics settings in
.I tacctl.yaml
survive.
.IP
The shell completion is rewritten only when its content changed (the output
says
.B Updated
or
.BR Unchanged ).
Sudoers drop-ins an administrator installed are refreshed when they are
this release's older text: the tiers file
.RB ( "Updated: tiers sudoers" )
and the group file of
.B "config sudoers install"
for the group its header names
.RB ( "Updated: sudoers for group" " \fIname\fR)."
An edited group file is left as it is, with a warning; one that says tacctl
wrote it but names no group
.B "config sudoers install"
accepts is left with a line saying so. None is created.
.IP
The upgrade ends with a red notice naming
.B "group reset engineer"
when the
.B engineer
group still carries the role preset of 0.2.2 (its Cisco rules or its Junos
sets). It ends with a red notice naming each setting, and the command that
puts the shipped value (or, for what the role preset of 0.2.3 stored, the
preset's) back, when a stored
.B privileges.operator
or
.BR commands.operator / commands.readonly ,
or the Junos deny sets of the preset of 0.2.3, still give an operator or a
viewer a command that can interrupt service.
.IP
An upgrade from a release before 0.2.5 also prints the lines a device
configured earlier keeps. An upgrade from a release before 0.2.7 names, once,
each scope that has a registered WTI unit and a plain IPv4 entry in the
permit list that applies to it (its own, else the global one), because a plain
entry permits every protocol and port on a WTI unit. The notice gives the
entries, up to five unit names per scope, the rule a WTI plan renders
.RB ( "iptables \-A INPUT \-s <cidr> \-j ACCEPT" ),
and the
.B <cidr>:tcp/22
form that keeps ssh only. It is recorded in
.I /var/lib/tacctl/wti\-plain\-acl\-noted
(a fresh install writes the file at once); later upgrades and
.B "config validate"
say nothing of it.
.IP
The upgrade never changes an override, except an operator or readonly list
that is exactly what 0.2.3 shipped, which it drops (on a RADIUS-only install
too). See
.BR UPGRADING .
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command upgrade
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-branch\fR \fI<name>\fR
The tacctl repo branch to install or upgrade from (default: the current one).
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command upgrade
.TP
.BI "rollback " version " [\-\-apply] [\-\-yes] [\-\-hosts]"
Prepare tacctl's state for the release before this one; run on the newer
release, before the older one is installed. A dry run by default: it lists
every step and every warning and changes nothing. The versions it takes are
.B 0.2.9
(the release before this one) and
.BR 0.2.3 ;
.BR 0.2.4 " to " 0.2.8
and
.B 0.2.2
and older are refused, with the way there (one release at a time: this
rollback,
.BR "tacctl upgrade \-\-branch 0.2.9" ,
then the rollback of that release), and so is any other word. Either target is
refused while a device's record says a confirmed commit of
.B "device config apply"
is
.B pending
or
.B unknown
(the refusal names each device and the time, and
.B "device config apply <name> \-\-check"
or
.B "device config forget <name>"
settles it).
.IP
.B "rollback 0.2.9"
.B \-\-apply
takes a snapshot, then removes from
.I tacctl.yaml
the keys a 0.2.9 binary refuses
.RB ( device.apply.transport
and the
.B netconf_scope
settings of
.BR "scope netconf" ;
the dry run warns, and
.B \-\-apply
refuses without
.B \-\-yes
while one is set) and from
.I devices.yaml
the
.B netconf
map of each device that has one
.RB ( "device netconf" ).
It leaves and lists, and never deletes, what 0.2.9 ignores: the
.B via
field of the
.B applied
parts of
.I /var/lib/tacctl/devices\-config.json
(0.2.9 reads the file without fields it does not know). Nothing else is
converted: 0.2.10 changed no sudoers text, and
.IR console.yaml ,
.IR store.yaml ,
the apply state directory and the hosts are in formats 0.2.9 reads.
.B \-\-hosts
is accepted and does nothing. The command ends with the next step,
.BR "tacctl upgrade \-\-branch 0.2.9" ,
and the audit line
.BR "rollback target=0.2.9" .
.IP
.B "rollback 0.2.3"
.B \-\-apply
takes a snapshot, then, in this order: removes from
.I tacctl.yaml
the keys 0.2.5 added
.RB ( device.config.max_concurrency ,
.BR device.config.transport ,
.BR device.config.timeout ,
.BR device.config.confirm_timeout ,
.BR device.apply.confirm_timeout ,
.BR device.apply.pause ,
.BR device.apply.transport ,
.BR netconf_scope.<scope> ,
.BR cisco.source_interface.<scope> ,
.BR breakglass_hold.<device> )
and any other key a 0.2.3 binary does not know; removes
.B settings.password_cache
from
.I console.yaml
and writes it the way 0.2.3 reads it (the cache is off); removes the
.B snmp
map, the
.B config_syntax
key and the
.B netconf
map of every device from
.IR devices.yaml
(devices use their scope's or the default's settings; a
.B legacy\-ssh
Cisco device is compared as IOS 12.x); and rewrites the sudoers drop-ins that are exactly the text 0.2.5 writes
.RI ( /etc/sudoers.d/tacctl\-tiers
and the one
.B "config sudoers install"
wrote) with the text 0.2.3 writes, after
.BR "visudo \-cf" :
they lose the
.B TACCTL_ASKPASS
alias and
.B env_keep
line and, in the per-tier file, the rows of
.BR "device config" ,
.BR "device snmp" ,
.BR "device config\-syntax" ,
.B "breakglass list"
and
.BR "console forget" .
A drop-in that was edited, or that
.B visudo
refuses, is left and named. It leaves, and lists,
.IR store.yaml ,
the devices' own SNMP credentials
.RI ( snmp/devices/ name .yaml ,
mode 0600) and the records of the configuration pulls
.RI ( /var/lib/tacctl/devices\-config.json
and
.IR device\-config/ ).
.IP
The dry run names what needs a decision, and
.B \-\-apply
refuses without
.B \-\-yes
while any applies: the settings that are dropped (a device's own SNMP
settings, a device's configuration syntax, a scope's Cisco source interface,
the
.B device.config
values, the password cache's tiers and lifetimes; they stay in the snapshot,
which only 0.2.5 restores). A break-glass hold that stands stops the
rollback, the dry run too, and names each held device with the way to release
it
.RB ( "breakglass release" ).
A management ACL entry with services
.RB ( mgmt_acl.permits
or
.BR scope_mgmt_acl.permits.<scope> ,
.BR 10.0.0.0/8:tcp/22 )
stops it the same way, and each entry is listed with the command that removes
it (0.2.3 reads those lists as plain CIDRs; rewriting an entry to its CIDR
permits every protocol and port from that source).
.B \-\-hosts
is accepted and does nothing. The command ends with the next step,
.BR "tacctl upgrade \-\-branch 0.2.3" .
A second
.B \-\-apply
changes nothing. See
.BR UPGRADING .
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command rollback
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-apply\fR
Take a snapshot, then convert the files the target cannot read (tacctl.yaml, sudoers; for 0.2.3 also console.yaml, devices.yaml).
.TP
\fB\-\-yes\fR
Answer yes to the confirmations.
.TP
\fB\-\-hosts\fR
Accepted; the hosts need nothing for this target (no host is synced).
.RE
