.B "host enroll " "{ [" user "@]" host " | \-\-local } [ \-\-method tacplus|radius ] [ \-\-scope " name " ] [ \-\-server " address " ] [ \-\-name " name " ] [ \-\-port " n " ] [ \-\-identity " file " ] [ \-\-build\-on\-host ] [ \-\-allow\-uid\-mismatch ] [ \-\-remove\-home ]"
Copy the
.B "config linux"
install script to a host over SSH, run it there as root, and register the
host. The remote copy of the script is deleted after the run; nothing is
registered if the remote run fails. Accounts are created, expired and deleted
as by
.B "host sync"
(including the question about removed users' homes, and
.BR \-\-remove\-home ).
The server address defaults to the source address of the route to the host.
.IP
The method is
.B tacplus
(pam_tacplus against the TACACS+ backend) or
.B radius
(the distribution's pam_radius_auth package against the RADIUS backend:
.B libpam\-radius\-auth
on Debian and Ubuntu,
.B pam_radius
from EPEL on the RHEL family, where the script enables EPEL when the host
lacks it and stops before any account or PAM change when it cannot install
the package). It is, in order:
.BR \-\-method ;
the method the host is registered with; the scope's
.BR "scope auth-method" ;
the one protocol the scope's
.B protocols
filter names;
.B "host default-method"
(default
.BR tacplus ).
The method's backend must be enabled and the scope must allow its
protocol. Re-enrolling a registered host with the other method switches it:
the first method's module, secret file and SELinux module are removed
(packages stay).
.IP
Without
.B \-\-scope
a registered host keeps its scope, and any other host goes into the scope that
answers its address (said, with the prefix). A host whose address no scope
covers is refused before anything changes, with the commands that add the
address to a scope or give the host a scope of its own
.RB ( "scope add linux\-" "name " "\-\-prefixes " addr "/32 \-\-secret generate" ).
Naming another scope for a registered host moves it: enroll names the users
whose accounts the move deletes and asks on a terminal; without one it stops
unless given
.BR \-\-yes .
tacctl never moves a host itself:
.BR "host sync" ,
.B "host list"
and
.B "config validate"
warn, with the enroll that moves it, when another scope answers the
address recorded for a host. The scope must be the one that answers the host's
requests (the first prefix that holds the address they come from): for
.B \-\-local
that address is 127.0.0.1, and a scope that does not cover it is refused
before anything changes; for another host, a scope that does not cover the
address its name resolves to is a warning (NAT may change it).
.B "host sync"
of this machine warns when its scope no longer covers 127.0.0.1.
.IP
ssh runs as the user who invoked
.BR sudo (8),
using their keys, and forwards neither their agent nor any port to the host;
the remote login must be root or able to sudo. A host whose address is one of
this machine's own (loopback and
.B 0.0.0.0
included) is refused unless it is enrolled with
.BR \-\-local .
That login (the provisioning account:
.I user
of the target, else the invoking user's name) must be a local account on the
host that does not authenticate through tacctl;
.B "host enroll"
refuses a user of tacctl, and
.B "host sync"
warns about an existing enrolment that uses one.
.B "tacctl ssh"
never uses it.
.IP
After a successful run the host's public key files are read over the same
ssh connection
.RB ( "cat /etc/ssh/ssh_host_*_key.pub" ),
and the target is scanned
.RB ( ssh\-keyscan
of the host and port, as root). Only the keys both reads hold are pinned in
the device registry for
.BR "tacctl ssh" ;
the output lists their
.B SHA256:
fingerprints. A key type the two reads give different keys for pins nothing:
both sets are printed and
.B "host hostkey\-mismatch"
is logged to syslog (auth.warning). A type only one read has is reported, not
pinned. Unreadable key files, or a host that does not answer the scan, pin
nothing, with a warning and the
.B "device hostkey"
command; the enrolment succeeds either way.
.B "host sync"
of a pinned host compares the key files with the pin and reports a
difference; it never re-pins. A host enrolled with
.B \-\-local
is not pinned.
.IP
For
.BR tacplus ,
pam_tacplus is built on this server, in a rootless
.BR podman (1)
container matching the host's OS release, cached, and shipped as a binary.
Debian, Ubuntu and RHEL-family hosts (RHEL, AlmaLinux, Rocky, CentOS
Stream, Oracle Linux; built against AlmaLinux of the same major release) are
supported. The host compiles the module from source instead when no image
matches its OS, its architecture differs, the container build fails, or
.B \-\-build\-on\-host
is given. A host that already has the module from the same source is not
touched. Requires
.B "config linux build"
to have been run. For
.B radius
none of this applies
.RB ( \-\-build\-on\-host
warns that it is not applicable).
.IP
A reject from the server is final. When the server does not answer (or the
host's shared secret is wrong), login falls through to the local password,
which tacctl's accounts do not have; local administrators and every other
local account outside
.B tac\-users
never reach the server. With the server silent a user of tacctl is refused
after about 5\(en6 s (tacplus) or 8\(en9 s (radius).
pam_radius_auth 2.0.x on Ubuntu 24.04 and Debian 12 neither sends nor checks
Message-Authenticator (BlastRADIUS, CVE-2024-3596); elsewhere tacctl adds
.BR require_message_authenticator .
Session accounting over RADIUS is sent only when the RADIUS
.B acct
listener is on the authentication port plus one, and never from Ubuntu
24.04 (malformed records).
