Manage a shared CIDR permit list stored under
.B mgmt_acl.permits
in
.I /etc/tacctl/tacctl.yaml
that drives the Cisco VTY-ACL, a commented Juniper lo0
firewall-filter example and the WTI IP Tables list emitted by
.BR "config cisco" ,
.B config juniper
and
.BR "config wti" .
Preserved across
.BR "tacctl upgrade" .
.BR list ,
.BR add ,
.B remove
and
.B clear
manage the permit list;
.B add
and
.B remove
accept one entry or a comma-separated list.
.B cisco-name
and
.B juniper-name
set the emitted ACL and filter names. With no arguments, it
prints usage, the current entry count, and both ACL names.
.RS
.PP
An entry is a CIDR, or a CIDR with the services it permits:
.IR cidr [: service [, service ...]]
(for example
.BR 172.16.100.0/23:tcp/22,udp/161,udp/162 ).
A service is
.BI tcp/ ports ,
.BI udp/ ports
or
.BR icmp ;
.I ports
is
.IR N ,
.I N\-M
or
.B *
(every port of the protocol). A plain CIDR, with no services, permits every
protocol and port from that source (on a WTI unit, one rule for every
protocol and port); write
.BI cidr :tcp/22
to permit ssh alone.
.IB cidr :*
and
.IB cidr :tcp/*,udp/*,icmp
are spellings of the plain entry. A token that starts with
.BR tcp/ ,
.B udp/
or
.B icmp
belongs to the entry before it, so
.B a,b:tcp/22,udp/161,c
is three entries; a service before any CIDR is refused. The CIDR is split from
its services at the first colon that is followed by
.BR tcp/ ,
.BR udp/ ,
.B icmp
or
.BR * ,
so an IPv6 CIDR is written as it is. Services are stored lower\-case, without
repeats, in the order tcp, udp, icmp and ports ascending; a protocol's
.B *
takes its other services; an entry holds at most 8 services; ports are 1 to
65535, a range has its low port first, and
.B icmp
takes no port. The same CIDR may be listed twice with different services; an
exact duplicate is a no\-op. Entries are kept sorted by the CIDR's broadcast
address, and by services for the same CIDR.
.B list
prints the services after the CIDR.
.B remove
with a CIDR and services removes that exact entry; with a CIDR alone it
removes every entry of that CIDR and says how many.
.PP
The WTI IP Tables list renders each service of an entry as its own rule
.RB ( "-p tcp -s " cidr " --dport 22" ,
.B "--dport 161:162"
for a range, no
.B --dport
for
.BR * ,
.B "-i eth0 -s cidr -p icmp"
for
.BR icmp ),
and a plain entry as
.BR "-s cidr" ;
ICMP from a network is still added unless its entry is plain or lists
.BR icmp .
A Cisco VTY access list and the Junos
.B permit-mgmt
term match a source only: a plain entry, and an entry whose services include
.B tcp/22
(alone, in a range or as
.BR tcp/* ),
are listed by their source; any other entry is not listed there, and the
walkthrough names it in a comment. The managed
.B mgmt-acl
sections, and so
.B "device config diff"
and
.BR "device config plan" ,
read the same filtered list.
.B "rollback 0.2.3"
is refused while any entry carries services.
