.BI "config cisco " [ "--scope " name "] [--legacy] [--protocol " tacacs | radius "] [--name " device "] [--server " address|name "] [--source " address "] [--snmp-location " text ]
Print a ready-to-paste Cisco IOS/IOS-XE configuration with the
server IP, shared secret, current group-to-privilege mappings, and
the VTY-ACL built from
.B config mgmt-acl
pre-filled. Over TACACS+ it has one
.B aaa accounting commands
line per privilege level a group uses and, once any group has command
rules, the per-level
.B aaa authorization commands
lines (see
.BR "group commands" ),
.B aaa authorization config-commands
and a comment that says not to set
.BR "aaa authorization console" .
Without
.BR --scope ,
it emits the default scope's secret (the header names the scope and lists
sibling scopes). With
.BR --legacy ,
it emits legacy IOS 12.x syntax
.RB ( "tacacs-server host" " and " "aaa group server \&... / server <ip>" ),
whatever the configuration syntax of the device named by
.B --name
is. With
.B --name
naming a registered Cisco device of the scope the syntax is that device's
.RB ( "device config\-syntax" ,
else what its last pull read from its version line, else the modern syntax and
one line on standard error that says so);
.B --legacy
is the explicit override. It differs from the modern output only in the server
block (the global
.B "tacacs-server host ... single-connection timeout 5 key ..."
line and
.B "server <ip>"
under the group, in place of
.B "tacacs server TACACS"
and
.BR "server name TACACS" ),
the local enable secret line
.RB ( "enable secret <PASSWORD>"
in place of
.BR "enable algorithm-type scrypt secret <PASSWORD>" ),
the break-glass hashes (type 5, not type 9), the NETCONF step (a comment) and
the Type 6 note (not available on IOS 12.x).
.IP
With
.B "--protocol radius"
the configuration is for the RADIUS backend instead (see
.B RADIUS DEVICE CONFIGURATIONS
below);
.B --legacy
is TACACS+ only.
.IP
The output is the standard Cisco block, the same lines in one order for every
Cisco device. Step 0 is a commented hardening checklist
.RB ( hostname ,
.BR "ip domain name" ,
.BR "no ip domain lookup" ,
.BR "service password-encryption" ,
.BR "service timestamps" ,
.BR "no service pad" ,
.BR "no ip source-route" ,
.BR "no ip http server" ,
.BR "no ip http secure-server" ,
the three
.B "ip ssh"
settings,
.BR "transport input ssh" ,
.BR exec-timeout ,
.BR "logging synchronous" )
and the local enable secret
.RB ( "no enable password" ,
.BR "enable algorithm-type scrypt secret <PASSWORD>" ;
.B "enable secret <PASSWORD>"
with
.BR --legacy ),
then the scope's break-glass accounts and one commented line that names
.B "tacctl breakglass hold"
as the way to use them while the server is up. Step 0 is site policy:
tacctl keeps no value for it and does not compare or plan its lines. Step 1 is the AAA standard in the order that
keeps the pasting session alive:
.B "aaa new-model"
first (nothing under
.B aaa
exists on IOS until it is set), the server block
.RB ( "tacacs server TACACS" )
and its group
.RB ( "aaa group server tacacs+ " <G>
.RB ", " "scope tacacs-group" ),
.B "ip tacacs source-interface"
when the scope sets one,
.B "aaa authentication login default"
and
.BR "aaa authentication login CONSOLE local" ,
.BR "aaa authorization exec default" ,
accounting,
.BR "aaa session-id common" ,
the
.B privilege exec level
lines, SNMP, the VTY access list, the commented NETCONF step,
.B "line con 0"
with
.B "login authentication CONSOLE"
and
.B "line vty 0 15"
with
.BR "login authentication default" .
The command authorization lines
.RB ( "aaa authorization commands " <level>
per level in use, then
.BR "aaa authorization config-commands" )
are Step 2, after a check: the session that pastes is a local user the server
does not know, and a server that answers
.B fail
for such a name never falls through to local, so every command of that session
would be denied. The walkthrough says to open a new session with the user's own
TACACS+ account, run
.BR "show privilege" ,
and only then type Step 2. The names are the plain ones
(server
.BR TACACS ,
group
.BR TACACS-GROUP ,
.BR VTY-ACL ,
.BR TACCTL-SNMP ,
and the console's list
.BR CONSOLE ).
The console list is local only, so that a local user can always log in there;
the default list asks the server first, and a reject does not fall through to
local. With the server up a local user on the console lands at privilege 1 (the
standard does not set
.BR "aaa authorization console" ,
and must not: it would put the console under the default lists, server first,
and defeat
.BR CONSOLE ).
The standard has no
.B "aaa authentication enable"
line, so
.B enable
uses the local enable secret and nothing else; a TACACS+ user lands at the level
the server's
.B priv-lvl
gives and does not need it. A list that asks the server is left out because
tacquito answers an enable request as a login with the user's own password: an
operator who typed
.B enable
and their own password would reach privilege 15 and could read
.BR "show running-config" .
The local enable secret is the one shared secret of the way up, on the vty and
on the console alike, while the server is up; command authorization at level 15
still asks the server, so an operator who knows it cannot configure, debug or
clear, and can read the running configuration. With
.B "--protocol radius"
the same standard is rendered where RADIUS has it (no command authorization,
.B "ip radius source-interface"
from the same setting, and no enable list either:
.B enable
uses the local enable secret). The source interface is
the one policy value the standard needs from the operator
.RB ( "config cisco source-interface" ):
unset, the walkthrough prints the line commented with that command and nothing
is rendered or compared. One typed inside
.B aaa group server
is an older per\-group form: the plan and the diff name it as such.
.IP
Every walkthrough of
.BR "config cisco" ", " "config juniper " "and " "config wti"
has an SNMP step, built from the settings of the scope with the default
beneath them (see
.BR "scope snmp" ):
read-only access for the tacctl server's own address first, the scope's
allowed client ranges in the order given, and everything else
.RB ( 0.0.0.0/0 )
refused last. On Cisco that is the standard access list
.B TACCTL\-SNMP
(ending in
.BR "deny any" )
bound to
.B "snmp-server community ... RO"
or to a v3 group and user (authPriv), with
.B snmp-server location
and
.BR contact ;
on Junos a
.B client-list
of the same name with
.BR "0.0.0.0/0 restrict" ,
the read-only community bound to it, or the v3 usm user with its vacm group
and view, and
.B "set snmp location|contact|description"
(the client list restricts communities only; the management filter of Step 4,
when it is rendered, has permit-snmp and deny-snmp terms for udp port 161 from
the same clients, which restrict v3 once the filter is applied to lo0); on WTI
the unit's SNMP menu in the walkthrough's style (item names and numbers as on a
WTI 8.10 unit: SNMP Access under /N: Enable, Version, Read Only, Contact, SNMP
Location, Read\-Only Community), with the client restriction in the udp port 161
lines of the unit's IP Tables list (Step 5). The
community or the v3 passphrases are printed for superusers and for engineers
(of their own scopes). A value that is not set (the scope's contact, a
device's location) is a commented placeholder, never text a paste would
apply, and the output ends with
.BI "Unfilled SNMP values: contact (tacctl scope snmp " scope " contact '<text>'), location (tacctl device location " name " '<text>')" .
An SNMP version unset in the scope and in the default makes the step a comment
saying SNMP is not configured.
The Cisco walkthrough ends with a commented
.B netconf-yang
step and its prerequisite (exec authorization), for superusers; an engineer's
says to ask one; IOS 12.x has none. The Junos walkthrough has a commented
.B "set system services netconf ssh"
step with the optional connection and rate limits and the check
.RB ( "ssh -p 830 -s" " user@device " netconf ),
and its management filter permits tcp port 830 next to ssh; the WTI unit has
no NETCONF. tacctl enables none of it itself unless the scope's or the
device's NETCONF setting says so
.RB ( "scope netconf" ,
.BR "device netconf" ),
and then the Junos step is live.
.B "--server"
.IR address|name
sets the address in the TACACS+ or RADIUS server lines; a host name must
resolve to an IPv4 address.
.B "--source"
.I address
is the address tacctl itself reaches the devices from (the first SNMP client
and the permit for ssh); the default for both is the address the route to the
Internet uses. Neither is stored; when they differ the output says which plays
which role. They combine with
.BR --staging .
