.SS Interactive shell \fB(tacctl shell)\fR
.TP
.BI "shell " "[ \-\-no\-history ] [ \-\-idle " min " ] [ \-\-space\-completion { on | off } ] [ \-\-password\-cache ] [ \-c " line " ]"
A prompt,
.BR "tacctl> " ,
where each line is a tacctl command without the
.B tacctl
in front:
.BR "user list" ,
.BR "scope show lab" .
The shell runs as the invoking user and holds no privilege: each line runs as
.B sudo
.RB [ \-n ]
.I tacctl words
with the terminal attached, so sudo's policy and log line and tacctl's tier
gate apply exactly as from a login shell.
.B \-n
is used for members of
.B tac-users
in the readonly or operator tier, or in no tier group (they have no local
password and their rules are NOPASSWD); a line that sudo refuses them and
that their tier's sudoers rules do not cover is reported as not permitted for
the tier. A member of
.B tac-superuser
runs plain
.BR sudo ,
which asks for the network password on the terminal when a line needs it
(once; sudo's cache then applies).
.B hash
and
.B completion
lines run without sudo.
For
.B host
lines the agent socket
.RB ( SSH_AUTH_SOCK )
is passed as it is when tacctl re-executes itself under sudo.
Messages and exit statuses are those of the command; after a command that
ends with a non-zero status the shell prints
.BR "[exit " \fIN\fB ] .
.IP
A line is split into words at blanks (space, tab); single quotes, double
quotes and backslash quote as in
.BR sh (1),
and nothing else is special: there are no variables, globbing, pipes,
redirections, command separators, command substitution or history expansion
(\c
.BR ; ,
.BR | ,
.BR $( ,
backticks,
.BR > ,
.B !
are ordinary characters of a word). A line is at most 4096 bytes.
A line pasted from the help or the documentation may start with
.BR tacctl ,
.B sudo tacctl
or
.BR /usr/local/bin/tacctl :
that first word (and the
.B sudo
before it) is dropped, once, and the rest runs, completes and is explained as
it would without it; only the first word counts, never one inside the line or
an argument.
A line that is only
.B tacctl
prints what
.B tacctl
with no arguments prints, the top-level usage, and ends with status 1. The
history keeps the line as it was typed.
The shell's own words are
.B help
(the top-level usage),
.BI "help " command
(the usage of that command's family),
.B history
(the lines entered, as stored),
.B console forget
(empties the password cache, see
.BR \-\-password\-cache ),
.B exit
and
.BR quit .
.IP
Editing keys: Left/Right, Home/End (Ctrl-A, Ctrl-E), Esc-b and Esc-f (or
Alt-Left, Alt-Right) by word, Backspace, Delete, Ctrl-K, Ctrl-U, Ctrl-W,
Ctrl-T, Ctrl-L; Up/Down (Ctrl-P, Ctrl-N) step through the history; Ctrl-R
searches it backwards as you type (Ctrl-R again: further back; Enter runs the
match; Ctrl-G gives the line back). Tab completes commands, sub-commands, flags
and names (users, groups, scopes, hosts, devices, ..., asked of
.B sudo \-n tacctl _completion-names
and kept for 5 seconds); a second Tab lists the matching words alone. ?
inserts nothing and shows help for the cursor's position: the words that can
come (\(lqPossible completions:\(rq), or after a command that takes arguments
its usage lines, its options and what comes next
(\(lqNext: <username> <group>\(rq, \(lqNext: <Enter> to run\(rq). Inside
quotes, or after a backslash, ? is typed as a character. A list of more than
40 entries asks first (\(lqShow all 45 devices? [y/N]\(rq): y shows it, any
other key (or Ctrl-C) prints how to narrow it and gives the line back. Help taller than the terminal is
paged (\(lq\-\- more (Space: page, Enter: line, q: quit) \-\-\(rq; q or Ctrl-C
stops). A paste is inserted as one line (its newlines become blanks, at most
4096 bytes) and runs only on Enter. Ctrl-C at the prompt discards the line;
Ctrl-C while a command runs ends the command, and the shell carries on.
Ctrl-Z and Ctrl-\e do nothing (there is no job control). Ctrl-D on an empty
line, like
.BR exit ,
leaves the shell.
.IP
Tab, ?, a typed space and the shell's top-level
.B help
list only the commands and sub-commands that the caller's tier can run
(the rows of the tier table): a readonly user does not see
.B store
or
.BR install ,
an operator does not see
.BR host ;
a superuser and a caller outside
.B tac-users
see everything. The list ends with \(lqShown: the commands the
.I tier
tier can run\(rq.
.BI "help " command
stays complete: it describes every verb of the family and ends with the tier
each one needs (with a note for the verbs whose sub-verbs the code splits:
.BR "scope secret" ,
.BR "scope staging" ,
.BR "host default\-method" ,
.BR "device import" ).
? after a verb typed by hand names the tier it needs
(\(lqNeeds the \fItier\fR tier.\(rq; after a command family the tier cannot run at
all, \(lqNeeds the \fItier\fR tier. help \fIfamily\fR describes it.\(rq). This is
display only: the tier gate and sudo remain the check. The shell learns a
.B tac-users
member's tier from the root side
.RB ( "sudo \-n tacctl _console-policy" ,
as the console does) when it starts, in the background and within 3 seconds, so
typing never waits for it; until the answer is in, or when it cannot be
learned, only the read-only commands are listed, and the next Tab, ? or space
asks again once 30 seconds have passed. A disabled tacctl user (the account has
no tier) is listed no command;
.BR help ,
.B exit
and
.B quit
remain. The usage printed by
.B tacctl
itself is not filtered.
.IP
The history is kept in
.I ~/.local/state/tacctl/history
(mode 0600, the newest 1000 lines).
A line is stored redacted: everything after
.BR \-\-secret ,
.B \-\-hash
or
.BR \-\-password ,
after
.B set
in a
.B secret ... set
line, after
.B import
when a value follows it, and after the word of a
.B passwd
line is replaced by
.BR \[u2026](redacted) ;
.B "scope secret lab set x"
is stored as
.BR "scope secret lab set \[u2026](redacted)" .
A recalled line that carries the mark is refused; type the value again.
Lines ended with Ctrl-C are not stored.
.B \-\-no\-history
keeps the history of the session in memory only.
.IP
.BR "\-\-space\-completion on" | off
(default on) makes a typed space work as on a Junos device. A space is
refused (nothing is inserted) at the start of the line, right after a space and
right before one. At the end of a word that is a command, a sub-command, a
fixed choice or a flag name, it completes the word when exactly one such word
starts with what is typed and adds the blank
.RB ( de
and a space give
.BR "device " );
when several do, they are listed once as ? lists them, without the question or
paging, and nothing is inserted (more than 40 words, or the console's
.BR list_max ,
print nothing). A word that is complete already
.RB ( user
while
.B user\-x
exists), a name (user, group, scope, host, device, file), free text, a typo and
a space in the middle of a word get the space as typed. Inside quotes, after a
backslash, in a Ctrl-R search, and with Tab and ?, nothing changes. A paste is
inserted as it is.
.B \-c
and batch input are not affected. The login console reads the setting from
.BR "console space\-completion" .
.B off
turns off the refusal of repeated spaces and the completion: every typed space
is a space.
.IP
.B \-\-password\-cache
keeps your network password in the memory of this shell for the session (see
.B "console password\-cache"
for the policy and the lifetimes): the first device pull asks for it once and
later pulls, diffs and
.B ssh
lines use it. It needs an interactive shell, and for a member of
.B tac-users
a tier that
.B "console password\-cache tiers"
names; a caller outside
.B tac-users
asks for it themselves. When the cache cannot start (memory cannot be locked,
a debugger is attached, no private directory is available for its socket) one
line says why.
.B console forget
empties it. The password is never written to a file, a log, an argument or the
environment.
.IP
.BI "\-\-idle " min
ends the shell after
.I min
minutes at the prompt without input (0, the default: never; at most 1440);
the time a command runs does not count.
.IP
.BI "\-c " line
runs one line and exits with its status; the line is stored in the history.
With standard input not a terminal (\c
.BR "tacctl shell < file" ),
the shell runs the lines of the input in order, with no prompt and no
history, and stops at the first line that ends with a non-zero status,
exiting with that status. The commands of such a batch get no standard input
(it is the script), so a command that asks a question cannot be answered
from it: use its
.B \-y
flag where it has one.
Over ssh, ask for a terminal to get the interactive shell:
.BR "ssh \-t " "host tacctl shell" ;
without
.B \-t
ssh allocates none, and the shell reads commands from standard input and
says so on standard error.
Bare
.B tacctl
on a terminal prints the usage and exits 1.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command shell
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-history\fR
Keep no history file for this session.
.TP
\fB\-\-idle\fR \fI<min>\fR
End the session after this many idle minutes at the prompt.
.TP
\fB\-\-space\-completion\fR \fIon|off\fR
A typed space completes a fixed word and never doubles (default on).
.TP
\fB\-\-password\-cache\fR
Keep your network password in this shell's memory (when your tier's policy allows).
.TP
\fB\-c\fR \fI<line>\fR
Run one line and exit.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command shell
.SH SCOPE MANAGEMENT
A
.I scope
is a named bundle of (client CIDR prefixes, shared secret), kept in the
store and shared by every backend: the same prefixes and secret serve
TACACS+ and RADIUS, unless the scope's
.B protocols
filter limits it. Each user's
.B scopes
list references one or more of these by name. A client is matched to the
scope with the most specific prefix that contains its address (tacquito
walks its rendered
.B secrets:
list, sorted by specificity, first match wins; FreeRADIUS takes the longest
prefix); authentication succeeds only when that scope is in the user's
list.
.PP
A fresh install has a single scope,
.BR lab ,
the least-privilege default. Add further scopes (for example
.BR prod ,
.BR edge ,
.BR dmz )
as needed and place users in them explicitly.
.PP
A change that writes what the scope's devices are rendered from
.RB ( "scope prefixes" ", " secret ", " mgmt\-acl ", " snmp ", " block ,
and the protocol, AAA order, timeout and group settings) says, in one line,
when at least one device of the scope has a stored pull that
.B "device config plan"
would now change:
.B "The configuration of devices in scope <name> would change: tacctl device config apply \-\-scope <name> \-\-check"
(a caller who is not a superuser is pointed to
.BR "device config plan \-\-scope" ).
It reads the stored pulls only: no login, no network, and nothing for a scope
with no pulled device.
.SS Top-level \fB(tacctl scope)\fR
.TP
.B scope list
One row per scope, with its prefixes joined as a comma list: names, prefix
counts, user counts and default marker. For the first-match routing view use
.BR "scope routing" .
For full per-scope detail (secret posture, per-scope knobs, user list) use
.BR "scope show " \fIname\fR .
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope list
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBreadonly\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope list
.TP
.B scope routing
Print every (scope, prefix) pair in first-match order, as rendered into
tacquito's
.B secrets[]
list (one row per scope-prefix pair, numbered by slice position). A
multi-prefix scope repeats its name on each prefix row; reading top to bottom
shows the order tacquito tries providers for a connecting client.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope routing
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope routing
.TP
.BI "scope show " name
Full detail for one scope: prefixes, users that reference it, the
shared secret's length and posture (never its value:
.BI "scope secret " name " show"
prints that), whether the scope is
the default, its protocols filter and auth-method, its vendor attributes
and tagged addresses (RADIUS).
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope show
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBengineer\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope show
.TP
.B "scope add " \fIname\fR " --prefixes " \fIcidr\fR[,cidr...] " [ --secret \fIvalue\fR | --secret generate ] [ --protocols \fIcsv\fR ] [ --vendor-attrs \fIcsv\fR ] [ --default ]
Create a new scope. Prefixes are required; each must not already
belong to another scope (overlap with a broader scope's supernet is
fine: the new scope's narrower prefix wins first-match).
.B --secret generate
produces a strong key.
.B --default
makes the new scope the default for
.BR "user add" .
.B --protocols
and
.B --vendor-attrs
set what
.B "scope protocols set"
and
.B "scope vendor-attrs enable"
would.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope add
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-prefixes\fR \fI<cidrs>\fR
(add) The scope's CIDRs, comma\-separated.
.TP
\fB\-\-secret\fR \fI<value>\fR
(add) The shared secret, or a generated one.
.TP
\fB\-\-protocols\fR \fI<csv>\fR
(add) Limit it to tacacs, radius (default: all).
.TP
\fB\-\-vendor\-attrs\fR \fI<csv>\fR
(add) RADIUS vendor attributes to send (cisco, juniper, wti).
.TP
\fB\-\-default\fR
(add) Make it the default scope.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope add
.TP
.BI "scope remove " "name " [ --force ]
Remove a scope from the store (and so from every backend's rendered
config). Refuses if any user references the
scope; with
.BR --force ,
strips the name from all affected users and removes the scope,
with a warning. Its vendor attributes and tagged addresses go with it. Every
.I tacctl.yaml
setting stored under the scope's name
.RB ( aaa.order ,
.BR exec_timeout ,
.BR tacacs_group ,
.BR radius_group ,
.BR scope_auth_method ,
.B scope_mgmt_acl.names.cisco|juniper
and
.BR scope_mgmt_acl.permits ),
its SNMP settings
.RB ( snmp_scope.\fIscope\fR.* )
and its SNMP credentials file are removed with it, so a new scope of that
name starts from the defaults.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope remove
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
(remove) Also take it out of the users that have it.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope remove
.TP
.BI "scope rename " "old new"
Rename a scope. Rewrites the scope in the store (protocols, vendor
attributes and tagged addresses included), every user's
.B scopes
reference, and
.B scope.default
in
.I tacctl.yaml
(if it pointed at
.IR old ),
and moves every
.I tacctl.yaml
setting stored under the old name to the new one.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope rename
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope rename
.TP
.BI "scope default " [ name ]
Show (no arg) or set (with arg) the default scope. Stored under
.B scope.default
in
.IR /etc/tacctl/tacctl.yaml .
Used by
.BR "user add"
when
.B --scopes
is omitted, and by
.BR "config cisco" " / " "config juniper" " / " "config wti"
when
.B --scope
is omitted.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope default
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope default
.TP
.BI "scope lookup " ip\fR|\fIcidr
Resolve an IPv4 / IPv6 address or CIDR to the scope that owns it, by the same
first-match walk tacquito's provider selector uses. Prints the matching prefix
and lists the scopes that also cover the query below the winner. An address
the scope has tagged with a vendor
.RB ( "scope devices" )
is shown with its tag.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope lookup
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope lookup
.SS Per-scope prefixes \fB(tacctl scope prefixes)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope prefixes " "scope " "list"
Show the scope's current prefixes.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope prefixes
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-all\fR
(remove) Remove every prefix of the scope, which removes the scope.
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
(remove \-\-all) Also take the scope out of the users that have it.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope prefixes
.TP
.B "scope prefixes " \fIscope\fR " add " \fIcidr\fR[,cidr...]
Add one or more CIDRs. Each is canonicalized
.RB ( 10.1.5.5/24 " -> " 10.1.5.0/24 ),
checked against the one-CIDR-per-scope invariant (rejected with the
owning scope's name if already claimed elsewhere), and added as its own
.B secrets[]
entry in the rendered
.IR tacquito.yaml ,
sorted globally by prefix specificity so first-match routes each client to the
narrowest scope that contains it (and its own RADIUS client).
.TP
.B "scope prefixes " \fIscope\fR " remove " \fIcidr\fR[,cidr...]
Remove one or more CIDRs. The last prefix of a scope cannot be removed
(use
.B "remove \-\-all"
or
.BR "scope remove" ),
and a prefix whose removal would leave a tagged address
.RB ( "scope devices" )
outside the scope is refused.
.TP
.BI "scope prefixes " "scope " "remove \-\-all " [ \-\-force ]
Empty the entire prefix list for the scope (with confirmation): the scope
and its per-scope settings are removed, so it vanishes from every rendered
config. The command refuses to proceed when users reference the scope
unless
.B \-\-force
is given, which strips the scope from those users.
.B \-\-all
takes no CIDRs, and
.B \-\-force
is only valid with it. A scope enrolled Linux hosts use is not removed
(move them first:
.BR "host move" ).
.TP
.B "scope prefixes " \fIscope\fR " move " \fIcidr\fR[,cidr...] " " \fIother\fR
Move the CIDRs from
.I scope
to
.I other
in one change, so no address they hold is left unanswered. The same checks
as remove and add apply. Enrolled hosts whose addresses another scope then
answers are named, with the
.B "host move"
that follows them; none is moved.
.SS Staging addresses \fB(tacctl scope staging)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope staging " "[ list | remove " address " ]"
A host or network device provisioned off-site for a scope, at a bench
address the scope does not cover
.RB ( "host enroll ... \-\-scope " "scope " "\-\-staging" ,
.BR "config cisco" | juniper | wti " \-\-scope " "scope " "\-\-staging " "ip " [ "\-\-name " device ]),
has that address added to the scope as a /32: the scope's own secret and
users answer it there, so nothing changes on the device when it is
installed in the scope's prefixes. The /32 is removed once the host (its
enroll or sync) or the registered device
.RB ( "device set" )
is seen at another address the scope covers.
.B list
(the default) shows them with where each is seen now;
.B remove
takes one out by hand.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope staging
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBengineer\fR. An engineer may run list for a scope of their own; the other verbs need the superuser tier.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope staging
.SS Per-scope secret \fB(tacctl scope secret)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope secret " "scope " "show"
Print the scope's raw shared-secret value plus its length and posture.
Superuser only; an engineer may read the secret of a scope of their own (each
read is logged), and may not set or generate one.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope secret
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBengineer\fR. An engineer may run show for a scope of their own; set and generate need the superuser tier.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope secret
.TP
.BI "scope secret " "scope " "set " value
Set the scope's key after validation (at least
.B config secret-min-length
chars, mixed character classes). With RADIUS enabled, a secret FreeRADIUS
cannot carry (a backslash together with a
.BR $ )
is refused;
.B "config render"
warns about one beyond the interoperability advice (63 printable
characters, no space). TACACS+ and RADIUS share the secret.
.TP
.BI "scope secret " "scope " "generate"
Generate a strong key with
.B openssl rand -base64 24
and apply it.
.SS Per-scope protocols \fB(tacctl scope protocols)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope protocols " "scope " "[list]"
Show which protocols serve the scope. Without a filter, every enabled
backend serves it.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope protocols
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope protocols
.TP
.BI "scope protocols " "scope " "set " protocol\fR[,protocol...]
Limit the scope to the named protocols
.RB ( tacacs ", " radius ).
A backend whose protocol is not listed leaves the scope out of what it
renders: for TACACS+, its prefixes, its secret and its users' grant of it are
absent from tacquito.yaml; for RADIUS, its prefixes are not clients. Refused
when the list leaves out the scope's
.BR "scope auth-method" .
.TP
.BI "scope protocols " "scope " "clear"
Remove the filter.
.SS Per-scope vendor attributes (RADIUS) \fB(tacctl scope vendor-attrs, tacctl scope devices)\fR
Over RADIUS an Access-Accept always carries
.B Service-Type
.RB ( Administrative-User
at priv-lvl 15, else
.BR NAS-Prompt-User ).
A vendor's privilege attribute is added only where the operator opted in:
.B Cisco-AVPair \(dqshell:priv-lvl=N\(dq
(cisco),
.B Juniper-Local-User-Name
(juniper),
.B WTI-Super
0\-3 (wti). An Access-Reject carries none. TACACS+ is not affected.
.TP
.BI "scope vendor-attrs " scope
Show the vendors enabled for the scope, or
.B "not sent"
(the state of a new scope).
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope vendor-attrs
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope vendor-attrs
.TP
.BI "scope vendor-attrs " scope " enable|disable " vendor\fR[,vendor...]
Enable or disable a vendor's attribute for every device of the scope that
is not tagged. There is no
.BR set ,
.B clear
or
.BR none :
nothing is sent until it is enabled. Stored on the scope in
.I store.yaml
.RB ( vendor_attrs );
the change re-renders the RADIUS backend and restarts it.
.TP
.BI "scope devices " scope " [list]"
List the scope's tagged addresses.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope devices
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBengineer\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope devices
.TP
.BI "scope devices " scope " set " "ip\fR|\fIcidr vendor"
Tag an address (a bare IP is a /32 or /128) or a range of the scope
with its vendor: it gets that vendor's attribute and no other, whatever
the scope enables. The address must be one
.B "scope lookup"
answers with this scope: inside a prefix of it, and inside no more
specific prefix of another scope. Stored on the scope in
.I store.yaml
.RB ( devices ).
.TP
.BI "scope devices " scope " unset " ip\fR|\fIcidr
Remove a tag.
.B "scope prefixes remove"
refuses to leave a tag outside the scope's prefixes (unset it first, or
add the new prefix before removing the old one); tags go with the scope
on rename and remove.
.SS Per-scope idle-session timeout \fB(tacctl scope exec-timeout)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope exec-timeout " scope " [" minutes ]
Show or set the idle-session timeout (in minutes) that
.B "config cisco --scope " \fIscope\fR
and
.B "config juniper --scope " \fIscope\fR
render into device configs. Cisco emits
.BR "exec-timeout \fIn\fR 0"
on
.B line con 0
and
.BR "line vty 0 15" ;
Junos emits
.BR "set system login idle-timeout \fIn\fR"
(for a registered device whose recorded release is before 16.1, which has no
such statement, the timeout is set on each login class instead).
Range 0..60. A value of
.B 0
disables idle expiry on both vendors. Default 60.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope exec-timeout
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope exec-timeout
.SS Per-scope mgmt-acl \fB(tacctl scope mgmt-acl)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope mgmt-acl " scope " " \
"<list|add|remove|clear|cisco-name|juniper-name>" " [" args "]"
Per-scope management ACL, with the subcommands of the global
.BR "tacctl config mgmt-acl" .
The effective permit list of a
scope drives the Cisco VTY-ACL, the Junos filter and the permit lines of
the WTI IP Tables list (Step 5 of
.BR "config wti" ).
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.BR list ,
.BR "add " <entry>[,...] ,
.BR "remove " <entry>[,...] ,
.B clear
— manage the per-scope permit list; an entry is a CIDR, or a CIDR with its
services
.RB ( 10.0.0.0/8:tcp/22,udp/161,icmp ;
see
.BR "config mgmt-acl" ),
and
.B remove
of a CIDR alone removes every entry of that CIDR. Render fallback is per-scope
-> global
.RB ( mgmt_acl.permits )
-> empty. An empty per-scope list prunes the override so the scope
falls back to the global; \fBclear\fR is the explicit way to revert.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR "cisco-name " [ label ] ,
.BR "juniper-name " [ label ]
— per-scope ACL / filter name. Fallback is per-scope -> global
.RB ( "config mgmt-acl cisco-name / juniper-name" )
-> shipped default (\fBVTY-ACL\fR for Cisco, \fBMGMT-ACL\fR for
Junos). A name starts with a letter, then
[A-Za-z0-9_-]; 1..63 chars.
.RE
.IP
Cisco puts the effective name in
.B "ip access-list standard <NAME>"
and the matching
.B "access-class <NAME> in"
on
.BR "line vty 0 15" ;
Junos puts it in
.B "set firewall family inet filter <NAME>"
and the commented lo0 apply.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope mgmt-acl
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope mgmt-acl
.SS Per-scope NETCONF service \fB(tacctl scope netconf)\fR
.TP
.BI "scope netconf " scope " [ show | enable | disable | leave ] [ \-\-connection\-limit " n " | none ] [ \-\-rate\-limit " n " | none ]"
The NETCONF service of the Junos devices of a scope.
.B enable
renders and plans
.BR "set system services netconf ssh" ;
.B \-\-connection\-limit
and
.B \-\-rate\-limit
(1 to 250; an enable's, and
.B none
removes one) add the limits, in which case the statements are the limits' own:
a limit's statement creates
.B ssh
by itself, and the device prints no bare
.B ssh
beside it.
.B disable
plans
.BR "delete system services netconf"
and drops the scope's limits.
.B leave
(the default, and what removes the scope's setting) plans and compares nothing
of the service, as before. A device's own setting
.RB ( "device netconf" )
wins over the scope's, and each limit layers on its own. The limits bound the
service's own port (830); tacctl reaches NETCONF through the SSH subsystem,
which they do not bound on Junos 25.4.
.B show
(or no word) prints the setting. The values are
.B netconf_scope.\fIscope\fR.{state,connection_limit,rate_limit}
in
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
written only when set; a rename moves them, a removal deletes them. The verb is
the superuser's.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope netconf
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-connection\-limit\fR \fI<n>\fR
(enable) The connection limit of the service, 1 to 250; none removes it.
.TP
\fB\-\-rate\-limit\fR \fI<n>\fR
(enable) The rate limit of the service, 1 to 250; none removes it.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope netconf
.SS Per-scope SNMP \fB(tacctl scope snmp)\fR
.PP
The SNMP settings of the devices of a scope. A setting the scope does not set
comes from the default
.RB ( "config snmp" ),
then the built-in; every
.B show
says which one a value is. The non-secret settings are
.B snmp_scope.\fIscope\fR.{version,port,timeout,v3.auth,v3.priv,contact,clients}
in
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
written only when set; the community and the v3 user with its passphrases are
the file
.IR /etc/tacctl/snmp/\fIscope\fR.yaml
(mode 0600 in a 0700 directory, the format of
.IR snmp.yaml ),
printed only by
.BR "show \-\-reveal" .
A scope's rename moves them, its removal deletes them. The SNMP lookup of
.B "device add"
and
.B "device check"
uses the credentials of the device's scope (the one that answers its address;
a device in no scope uses the default). The verbs that set or clear anything,
and
.BR test ,
are the superuser's.
.TP
.BI "scope snmp " scope " show [ \-\-reveal ]"
The effective version, port, timeout, protocols, community or v3 user, contact
and allowed clients of the scope, each with where it comes from
.RB ( scope ,
.BR default ,
.BR built\-in ).
.B \-\-reveal
prints the credentials; an engineer may reveal the scopes of their own, and the
reveal is logged as
.BR "secret\-read kind=snmp" .
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope snmp
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBengineer\fR. An engineer may read the settings of a scope of their own (show [\-\-reveal], version, port, timeout, contact, clients list); every setter, clear and test needs the superuser tier.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-reveal\fR
Print the community or the v3 passphrases (an engineer: their own scopes; logged).
.TP
\fB\-\-clear\fR
Remove the contact.
.TP
\fB\-\-stdin\fR
(community, v3\-user) Read the secrets from stdin, one per line.
.TP
\fB\-\-auth\fR \fIsha|sha256\fR
The authentication protocol: sha (HMAC\-SHA\-96) or sha256 (HMAC\-SHA\-256\-192) (default: the default's, else sha).
.TP
\fB\-\-priv\fR \fI<value>\fR
The privacy protocol: AES\-128 (the only one).
.RE
