applies again.
.TP
.BI "scope snmp " scope " test " address|device
Read the sysName of one address (or registered device) with the scope's
settings.
.SS Per-scope configuration blocks \fB(tacctl scope block)\fR
.PP
A configuration block is vendor syntax that a scope, or one device, owns beside
its structured settings, kept as a text file of its own and held to a grammar.
The vendors with blocks are
.B juniper
(the blocks
.B snmp
and
.BR system )
and
.B cisco
(IOS and IOS\-XE: the blocks
.B snmp
and
.BR system ) ;
a WTI unit has none. Junos and IOS are never translated into each
other, so a scope with both has a file for each.
.PP
The Junos
.B snmp
block is the SNMP that the scope's settings do not hold: communities, their
client lists and trap groups. The Junos
.B system
block is the login message, domain name and search list, time zone, name
servers and NTP. A Junos block is
.B set
statements and nothing else, one per line, with
.B #
comment lines and blank lines; the login message is the one statement that is
a heredoc,
.BR "set system login message <<'EOF'" ,
its lines, and
.BR EOF .
A
.B delete
line says that what it names is not managed on this device: it is kept, never
rendered, and in a device's block takes the scope's object of that name out for
that device. A Junos block may also state
.B "set snmp trap\-options source\-address <address>|lo0"
(one; an IPv4 or IPv6 address or the keyword
.BR lo0 ):
a value of the device's kind, set over the device's own value and never
deleted, which the check notes as the same address for every device of the
scope.
.PP
The IOS
.B snmp
block is configuration lines, one per line, with
.B #
and
.B !
comment lines and blank lines:
.B "snmp\-server community <string> RO|RW [<acl>]"
(the access list is a name, never a number),
.B "snmp\-server host <address> [vrf <name>] [informs|traps] [version 1|2c] <community> [udp\-port <n>] [<type> ...]"
(a trap host, one object per address, vrf, kind, udp\-port and community;
.B informs
need
.BR "version 2c" ;
SNMPv3 is refused),
.B "snmp\-server enable traps [<type> [<subtype> ...]]"
.B "snmp\-server trap\-source <interface>"
(one, the interface written in full, as the device stores it:
.BR Loopback0 )
and
.B "ip access\-list standard <NAME>"
with
.B "permit <address> [<wildcard>]"
(also
.B "permit host <address>"
and
.BR "permit any" )
under it. A
.B no
line (of a community, a host, an
.B enable traps
line, the trap source or a list) says what it names is not managed on this
device, as
.B delete
does. The IOS
.B system
block is configuration lines too, with the login banner as a heredoc:
.BR "banner login <<'EOF'" ,
its lines, and
.BR EOF ;
.B "ip domain name [vrf <name>] <name>"
and
.B "ip domain list [vrf <name>] <name>"
(the older spellings
.B ip domain\-name
and
.B ip domain\-list
are read as these);
.B "clock timezone <zone> <hours> [<minutes>]";
.B "ip name\-server [vrf <name>] <address> [<address> ...]";
.B "ntp server [vrf <name>] <address\-or\-name> [prefer] [version <1\-4>] [key <n>] [source <interface>]";
.B "ntp authentication\-key <n> md5 <string>";
.B "ntp trusted\-key <n>"
and
.BR "ntp authenticate" .
The banner is typed as IOS's multi\-line banner
.RB ( "banner login <d>" ,
the text,
.B <d>
alone on a line), where
.B <d>
is the first of
.B "^ ~ % # @ $ &"
that its text does not contain; a banner is at most 20 lines of 80 characters,
ASCII, with no tab or control character. A statement that names a vrf is
another object than the same statement without it (the domain name is one per
vrf), a vrf's name is letters, digits,
.BR . ,
.B _
and
.BR \- ,
and an IPv6 NTP server is not in a block.
.PP
The blocks of a scope are the files under
.IR /etc/tacctl/blocks/\fIscope\fR/\fIvendor\fR ,
and a block is in force only while
.I tacctl.yaml
names it
.RB ( blocks.\fIscope\fR.\fIvendor\fR ).
A block with an error is never rendered in part: every device of its vendor it
reaches is refused.
.PP
The communities and trap groups of a Junos
.B snmp
block become objects tacctl owns: the plan of
.B "device config plan"
sets what a device lacks and deletes a client or target of those objects that
the block does not state, under the object's real name, and the device's other
communities are kept and listed as unmanaged. The Junos
.B system
block is additive: a statement is set when it is missing, a single value is
replaced in place, and nothing the device holds is deleted.
.PP
The IOS
.B snmp
block owns a community (its line is retyped as the block states it, access and
access list; another community of the device is kept and listed as unmanaged)
and a standard access list it defines (whole: an entry the block does not state
is removed with its own
.B no
line); a list it only names is the device's own and is kept. Trap hosts (an
entry of an address, vrf, kind, udp\-port and community, as IOS keeps it, so
one address as traps and as informs is two hosts; the version and the types are
replaced in place when they differ, and IOS leaves out the default version,
kind and port) and
.B enable traps
lines (one counts as present when the device enables it, in the expanded form
it prints; a line with no type is present when the device has any) are only
ever added, and what the device holds besides is its own. The trap source is
one value, retyped in place when the device's differs (the interface is compared
without case) and kept while the block states none. The IOS
.B system
block is additive like the Junos one: a missing statement is set, a single value
(the banner, the domain name, the time zone, an NTP server's options, a trap
source) is replaced in place, a list entry is only added, and nothing the device
holds is removed. An
.B ntp authentication\-key
string is typed only where the device has no key of that number (keys compare
by number alone). A device in the IOS 12.x syntax stores
.B "ntp server <host> version 3"
without the option, so its walkthrough, managed section and plan type and
compare the line without
.BR "version 3" ;
every other option and version stays. The plan types the NTP lines in the order IOS needs: the
keys, the trusted keys, the servers and
.B ntp authenticate
last. The banner is compared by its size and a digest, never by its text, and a
banner replaced by the plan cannot be typed back by the back\-out: the back\-out
names
.B configure replace
and the saved configuration. IOS has no confirmed commit, so the plan of an IOS
device is pasted line by line and undone with a back\-out of
.B no
lines (a trap host's is
.B no
and its line up to its
.BR udp\-port ,
without the types). The report marks the section of an IOS block in the plan as
not checked on a device. A statement that names a vrf is another object than
the same statement without it, and a host of another vrf, kind or port is
another entry; the device's own stay
.RB ( "ok (+N unmanaged)" ).
tacctl does not create a vrf: the device refuses a block that names one it
lacks when the plan is pasted.
.PP
A block holds community strings, so every output but
.B "show \-\-reveal"
shows marks instead:
.B <secret:N>
for a community string,
.B <group:N>
for a trap group's name,
.B <secret>
for an NTP key,
.B "<message: N lines, M characters>"
for the login message or banner, and a comment line is never shown.
.PP
A device's own block is laid over its scope's per object: a community (by its
string), a trap group (by its name) or a trap\-options leaf of the device
replaces the scope's of the same key whole, one it adds is added and the
scope's others stay; in the system block a single value is the device's and a
list entry is added or, when it has the same value, replaced. The
management filter's
.B permit\-snmp
term permits the resolved block's unrestricted IPv4 clients.
.TP
.BI "scope block " scope " {juniper|cisco} list"
The blocks of the scope: for each, whether it is in force, has no file, or is a
file that
.I tacctl.yaml
does not name (and is ignored), its size, and what it states (communities and
trap groups, or statements and the login message's lines, or the number of
errors), then the findings: a file no list names and a name with no file.
Nothing is secret. An engineer may list the scopes of their own.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command scope block
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBengineer\fR. An engineer may read the configuration blocks of a scope of their own (list, show [\-\-reveal], check); set, remove, edit and adopt need the superuser tier.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-reveal\fR
Print the strings (an engineer: their own scopes; logged).
.TP
\fB\-\-file\fR \fI<path>\fR
(check, set) The text to look at: a regular file of at most 256 KiB, read as you.
.TP
\fB\-\-against\fR \fI<device>\fR
Plan the device of this scope with this text in force and print the statements of its section (from its last pull; nothing is written and no device is reached).
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
(set, remove, edit, adopt) Answer yes to the question (a block read from standard input needs it).
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command scope block
.TP
.BI "scope block " scope " {juniper|cisco} show " block " [ \-\-reveal ]"
The block as it is in force, with its secrets shown as marks (a comment is never
shown, and a block with errors is refused: run
.BR check ).
.B \-\-reveal
prints the file as stored; an engineer may reveal the scopes of their own, and
every reveal is logged, the superuser's too, as
.BR "secret\-read kind=block name=" \fIscope\fR/\fIvendor\fR/\fIblock\fR .
.TP
.BI "scope block " scope " {juniper|cisco} check " block " [ \-\-file " path " | \- ] [ \-\-against " device " ]"
Lint the file in force, the file
.I path
(a regular file of at most 256 KiB, read as the caller) or standard input
.RB ( \- ):
every finding with its line, the objects that become owned, the number of
statements that carry a secret, the difference from the file in force (elided;
a changed string shows as the same mark removed and added), and a block
community that is the scope's own community, which a device cannot hold twice
and is refused without printing it. Exit status 1 when there is an error.
.B \-\-against
plans the named device of the scope (of the vendor of the block) with the text in
force and prints the statements for the block's section, from the device's last
pull: nothing is written, no back\-out is kept and no device is reached. A
device that has not been pulled is told to be pulled first, and a system block
that was not in force at the last pull is told to be set and pulled.
.TP
.BI "scope block " scope " {juniper|cisco} set " block " { \-\-file " path " | \- } [ \-y ]"
Superuser only. Check the text as
.B check
does (an error refuses it and nothing is written), show the owned objects and
the difference, ask (\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR, answers yes; a text read from
standard input needs it unless standard input is a terminal), take the
configuration snapshot, write the file (0600, atomically) and put the block in
force in
.IR tacctl.yaml .
Nothing is restarted: a block reaches a device through
.BR "device config plan" .
A text that states nothing (comments and blank lines) is refused; the same text
as the file in force is no change. A change that cannot be made because
.I tacctl.yaml
cannot be read or written is refused before anything is. Logged as
.BR "block\-set name=" \fIscope\fR/\fIvendor\fR/\fIblock\fR .
.TP
.BI "scope block " scope " {juniper|cisco} remove " block " [ \-y ]"
Superuser only. Take the block out of force and delete its file (a file no list
names is removable too), after a question (\fB\-y\fR answers it) and a
snapshot. Nothing is deleted from a device: what the block set is no longer
rendered, and the next compare lists those statements as the device's own,
kept. Logged as
.BR block\-remove .
.TP
.BI "scope block " scope " {juniper|cisco} edit " block " [ \-y ]"
Superuser only, and only at a terminal (a refusal without one changes nothing).
Open the block in the editor of the invoking user, as
.BR visudo (8)
and
.BR sudoedit (8)
do:
.BR VISUAL ,
else
.BR EDITOR ,
else
.BR editor ,
else
.BR vi .
A value may carry arguments
.RB ( "emacs \-nw" )
and a name is looked up in
.BR PATH ;
a path must be absolute. The editor runs as the invoking user, never as root,
on a private copy (mode 0600, named for the block) in a directory of its own
(mode 0700, made under
.I /var/tmp
and handed to that user) with the user's
.BR HOME ,
.BR TERM ,
.BR LANG ,
.B LC_*
and
.BR PATH ,
.B VISUAL
and
.B EDITOR
as given, and nothing of sudo's or tacctl's; the directory, swap and backup
files included, is removed afterwards on every path, a stop signal included.
The seed is the file in force; for a
block that does not exist, the grammar of the block and an example, all as
.B #
lines, and for a device's new block the scope's objects as
.B "# set ..."
lines to remove the
.B "# "
of where the device differs. What the editor leaves is read back as that user (a
regular file of at most 256 KiB that is theirs; a link or anything else is
ignored), checked as
.B set
checks it, and installed as
.B set
installs it: the owned objects and the difference, the question
(\fB\-y\fR answers it), the snapshot, one atomic write and the entry in
.IR tacctl.yaml .
A text that fails the check is offered back to the editor, up to five times,
with the errors as
.B "# tacctl:"
lines at the top, numbered as the editor's window is; those lines are removed
before any text is read and never reach the file. An editor that exits
non\-zero, an empty text, a text that states nothing and an edit that changes
nothing write nothing. In the login console an editor can start a shell, so
an edit is open to the tiers of
.B "console system\-shell tiers"
(never the engineer), and a refusal is logged
.BR "block edit DENY" .
The editor has to stay in the foreground until the text is saved (a graphical
editor needs its wait option, such as
.B "code \-\-wait"
or
.BR "gvim \-f" ;
one that returns at once leaves tacctl an unchanged text). Logged as
.BR "block\-edit name=" \fIscope\fR/\fIvendor\fR/\fIblock\fR .
.TP
.BI "scope block " scope " {juniper|cisco} adopt " device " " block " [ \-y ]"
Superuser only, at a terminal, with the rules of
.BR edit .
Read the registered
.I device
of the scope (of the vendor of the block) live, with the caller's login, for its
statements of the hierarchy the block is for
.RB ( "show configuration snmp | display set" ,
or
.BR system ;
for an IOS device
.BR "show running\-config | include ^snmp\-server" ,
then
.B "show running\-config | section ip access\-list standard <NAME>"
for each list a community names, and the whole configuration once when the
image does not know
.BR "| section" ;
for an IOS system block
.BR "show running\-config | include ^(banner|ip domain|ip name\-server|clock timezone|ntp )"
and the banner's lines with
.BR "show running\-config | section ^banner login" ),
and open them in the editor as the draft to prune. The read is that of
.BR "device config pull" :
the password from the shell's cache or the terminal, the transport and timeout
of
.BR "config devices" ,
the pinned host key and the same words for a failure; one device, one login,
one command. Nothing of the device's text is stored beyond the editor's private
copy, which is removed. The read is logged as a reveal is,
.BR "secret\-read kind=block\-adopt name=" \fIscope\fR/\fIvendor\fR/\fIblock\fR
(never a value), and
.BR "block adopt\-read" .
The draft holds the statements the grammar of the block takes, minus what
tacctl renders itself (the scope's own community and its client list, the v3
settings, a location or contact it renders) and the device's identity
.RB ( "snmp name" ", " engine\-id ;
on IOS the contact, location and engine id). An IOS community that names a
numbered list, a view or an IPv6 list, and a list with a deny entry, cannot be
adopted and is named by a
.B "# left out:"
line.
A community string or trap group name the login sees masked
.RB ( "/* SECRET\-DATA */" ,
.BR <masked> ,
asterisks) or only as the device's ciphertext cannot be adopted, and an NTP
authentication key is always ciphertext: each such statement is named by a
.B "# left out:"
line with its shape and no value. The system block's draft converts the
device's login message (an IOS device's login banner) to the heredoc; the
device's own trap source is taken into an snmp block's draft. A block of that
name that exists is opened first, whole, and the adopted statements follow
under a
.B "# adopted from"
.IR device " at " time
comment with the counts; an object the file already has with other statements
is left as it is and counted. A draft with nothing adoptable opens no editor
and says why; one pruned to nothing writes nothing. The rest is that of
.BR edit ,
logged
.BR "block\-adopt name=" \fIscope\fR/\fIvendor\fR/\fIblock\fR .
