.SH DEVICE REGISTRY
The
.I device registry
gives names to the network devices that authenticate against this server, so
that they can be listed, looked up and acknowledged by name. It is kept in
.I /etc/tacctl/devices.yaml
(mode 0600, root only) and never touches
.IR store.yaml :
a device's scope and vendor tag are looked up from the store each time they are
shown, and registering a device renders nothing and restarts
nothing. Every write takes a snapshot first (see
.BR "backup list" ).
.PP
A device is found by name or by its registered address; names are compared
without regard to case. Enrolled Linux hosts
.RB ( "tacctl host" )
share the namespace: they are listed and shown as
.B linux
devices, read-only, and a device cannot take a host's name (nor
.B "host enroll \-\-name"
a device's). An address is registered at most once. An address that is not
registered is not found, even when a scope covers it.
.PP
A name that is a factory or image default (\c
.BR switch ,
.BR router ,
.BR cisco ,
.BR ubuntu ,
.B ip\-10\-0\-0\-1
and the like; matched without regard to case) is refused with the command that
gives the device a name of its own, unless
.B \-\-allow\-generic
is given; the device then carries a
.B generic-name
notice. A host already enrolled under such a name is not refused, and carries
the notice. The built-in list can be extended with
.B generic_names:
(regular expressions matched against the whole name) in
.IR devices.yaml .
.PP
Host keys are pinned when a device is registered: tacctl reads the keys the
device offers with
.BR ssh\-keyscan (1)
(as root, outbound, without logging in; for a
.B legacy\-ssh
device that
.B ssh\-keyscan
cannot read, because it cannot do the SHA-1 key exchange and CBC ciphers
such a device offers, with tacctl's own ssh client, which stops at the host key,
before authentication), keeps them in
.IR devices.yaml ,
and prints their
.B SHA256:
fingerprints to be compared with the device console. Enrolled hosts are pinned
by
.B "host enroll"
and
.BR "host sync" .
Every registry write regenerates
.IR /var/lib/tacctl/ssh/known_hosts ,
which lists exactly the pinned keys under each device's name, for
.BI "ssh \-o UserKnownHostsFile=/var/lib/tacctl/ssh/known_hosts \-o GlobalKnownHostsFile=none \-o HostKeyAlias=" name " \-o StrictHostKeyChecking=yes" .
A pin changes only through
.BR "device hostkey accept" " or " "device hostkey set" ,
never by itself.
.PP
What the daemons saw of each device is kept in the
.I seen cache
.RI ( /var/lib/tacctl/devices\-seen.json ),
which
.B "device scan"
fills from the logs: the tacquito journal (the
.BI "accepting user [" user "] from [" address "]"
and
.B "failed to validate the user"
lines, the
.B "bad secret detected for ip"
and
.BI "remote [" address "] has no secret providers"
errors, and at log level 30 the
.B "prefix secret provider matches remote"
lines) and FreeRADIUS's
.I tacctl\-auth.log
with its rotations. For each address it keeps the first and last sighting,
the count, the last user, outcome and backend, and the last NAS-Identifier.
.B list
and
.B show
read the cache only, and say how old it is. An interactive session of the
console or of
.B "tacctl shell"
for the operator tier and up starts
.B "device scan \-\-if\-stale \-\-quiet"
in the background as it opens, so the logins are in the cache by the time the
list is looked at and nothing a person types waits for it. That scan does
nothing when the cache is younger than five minutes or another scan holds it
(sessions that start together run one), reads a device's host keys again only
when that device's last scan is a day old, goes on after the session ends and
prints nothing. Seen data an hour old or more is said so, with the command to
bring it up to date, in the footer of
.B list
and in the
.B Last seen
line of
.BR show .
.PP
Notices are computed each time they are shown; only acknowledgements are
stored. Besides the registry's own
.RB ( generic-name ,
.BR hostkey-unpinned ,
and for an enrolled host
.BR address\-changed :
its address changed between two enrolments or syncs),
the seen cache raises
.B ambiguous-nas-id
(one NAS-Identifier sent from several addresses; a fully qualified host name
on each device tells them apart),
.B generic-nas-id
(a device identifying itself by a generic name),
.B name-mismatch
(a NAS-Identifier other than the registry name or the hostname, either in full
or by its first label, as
.B sw1
for
.BR sw1.site-a.example ;
informational),
.B duplicate-address
(two entries at one address, or an address identifying as another entry),
.B identity-changed
(the NAS-Identifier an address sends changed: a replaced or reset unit), and
the host-key re-scan of
.B "device scan"
and
.B "device check"
raises
.B hostkey-changed
(no pinned key offered, or another key of a pinned type; it cannot be
acknowledged, only
.B "device hostkey accept"
or
.B set
clears it),
.B hostkey-added
(a new key type, the pins unchanged) and
.B hostkey-unreachable
(no key could be read; with the last good scan). A scan never changes a pin.
Notices appear in the scan output,
.BR "tacctl status" ,
.BR "device list" ,
.B "device notices"
and
.B "device show"
(which counts the acknowledged ones;
.B \-\-all
on either lists them, marked).
.PP
Reading verbs
.RB ( list ,
.BR show ,
.BR notices ,
.BR ssh\-config )
and
.B "tacctl ssh"
are open to the read-only and operator tiers, which see only the devices and
hosts whose scope is one of their own; an entry no scope covers is seen by
administrators only.
.BR export ,
.BR scan ,
.BR discover ,
.B check
and
.B "list \-\-scan" | "\-\-probe"
are operator-level (the scan output's notices are filtered the same way;
.B discover
lists every address). Everything else needs an administrator.
.SS Device registry \fB(tacctl device)\fR
.TP
.B device
With no subcommand (or with
.BR help ),
prints a usage summary and exits 0.
.TP
.BI "device list " "[ word ] [ \-\-name " text " ] [ \-\-ip " address|cidr|text " ] [ \-\-vendor " vendor " ] [ \-\-scope " scope " ] [ \-\-stale ] [ \-\-unconfigured ] [ \-\-scan ] [ \-\-probe ] [ \-\-json ]"
One row per registered device, then per enrolled host, sorted by name
(upper and lower case alike): name, address, vendor,
scope, state, last seen, by, via, config, and the open notices.
.B \-\-name
keeps the names that contain the text,
.B \-\-ip
the addresses equal to an address, inside a CIDR, or containing a text
.RB ( 10.20. ),
.B \-\-vendor
the vendors named, and
.B \-\-scope
the devices that scope answers; each takes one value or a comma list (any of
them matches), and different options must all match. A
.I word
alone keeps the entries whose name or address contains it or whose vendor or
scope it is. The heading says how many of the visible entries are shown. The config
column (the operator tier's: a read-only user's list has none, in the table
or in the JSON) is the state of the device's last configuration pull
.RB ( "device config pull" ):
.BR ok ,
.BR differs ,
.B never
or
.BR failed ,
from the pull's record without rendering; it is
.B \-
for a host, a vendor
.B other
and a device no scope covers. The state is
.B configured
when a scope's prefixes cover the address (a host: when its scope exists) and
.B unconfigured
otherwise.
.B \-\-unconfigured
lists only those;
.B \-\-stale
lists the devices whose last sighting is older than
.BR stale\-days ,
which are also marked
.BR stale .
The last seen column is the time of the last sighting,
.BI "rejected " time
when the last exchange was a refusal (with
.B "(bad secret)"
or
.B "(no scope)"
when the device's packets could not be read or no scope covers it), and
.B never
for an address no scan has seen; with no seen data at all the columns print
.BR \- .
The footer says when the seen data was read.
.B \-\-scan
runs
.B "device scan"
first.
.B \-\-probe
adds a
.B REACH
column: a TCP connect to each entry's ssh port, 3 seconds, all at once
.RB ( open ,
.BR closed ,
.BR timeout ,
.BR unreachable ).
A timeout may be a false alarm when this server has no route to the
management ports.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command device list
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBreadonly\fR. The CONFIG column (and config, release, model and serial of \-\-json) is the operator tier's; a read\-only user's list has none.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-stale\fR
Only the devices not seen for stale\-days.
.TP
\fB\-\-unconfigured\fR
Only the devices no scope's prefixes cover.
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fR \fI<text>\fR
Only names that contain the text (a comma list: any of them).
.TP
\fB\-\-ip\fR \fI<addr|cidr|text>\fR
Only addresses equal to, inside, or containing it (an address, a CIDR or a text; a comma list).
.TP
\fB\-\-vendor\fR \fIcisco|juniper|wti|other\fR
Only that vendor: cisco, juniper, wti, other or linux (a comma list).
.TP
\fB\-\-scope\fR \fI<scope>\fR
Only the devices that scope answers (a comma list).
.TP
\fB\-\-scan\fR
Scan the logs first (as 'device scan').
.TP
\fB\-\-probe\fR
Add REACH: a TCP connect to each ssh port (3 s).
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
(list, show, export, check) Print JSON.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command device list
.TP
.BI "device show " "name|address " [ \-\-all ] " " [ \-\-json ]
One device or host in full: address, hostname, vendor, port, legacy ssh,
for a Cisco device its configuration syntax (the setting and, for
.BR auto ,
what the last pull resolved it to),
description, the scope that answers the address and the prefix that routes it
(and the scopes it shadows), the vendor tag that covers it, the pinned host keys,
the last and first sighting with the count and the NAS-Identifier the
device sends, the last pull's configuration (when, by whom, transport, NETCONF
probe result, overall result and the state of each managed section, with
.B "ok (+N unmanaged)"
for one that holds only statements of the device's own; for a WTI unit also its
command menu version, Site ID and the number of other sessions at the
pull), then what the device reported of itself at that pull as
.BR Release: ,
.B Model:
and
.B Serial:
rows (the OS release, the platform and the chassis serial number; a row is left
out when the pull did not read it; a WTI unit has a release only), and
the open notices with a count of the acknowledged ones
.RB ( \-\-all
lists them too, marked). It warns when the
vendor tag and the registered vendor disagree.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command device show
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBreadonly\fR. The Configuration, Release, Model and Serial rows (and config, release, model and serial of \-\-json) are the operator tier's; a read\-only user's output has none.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
(list, show, export, check) Print JSON.
.TP
\fB\-\-all\fR
The acknowledged notices too.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command device show
.TP
.BI "device add " "[ name ] address " "[ \-\-vendor cisco|juniper|wti|other ] [ \-\-hostname " dns " ] [ \-\-port " n " ] [ \-\-description " text " ] [ \-\-snmp\-location " text " ] [ \-\-legacy\-ssh ] [ \-\-config\-syntax auto|ios12|modern ] [ \-\-host\-key SHA256:" fingerprint " | \-\-no\-host\-key ] [ \-\-no\-lookup ] [ \-\-pull ] [ \-\-allow\-generic ]"
Register a device. A name is letters, digits,
.RB ` . ` ,
.RB ` _ `
and
.RB ` - ` ,
starting with a letter or digit, at most 253 characters, each dotted part at
most 63: a fully qualified host name such as
.B sw1.site-a.example
is a name. The vendor defaults to
.B other
(it only drives the ssh profile and does not tag the address in the scope).
The address is stored canonically (IPv6 in compressed lower-case form) and may
carry no prefix length. A name or address that is taken, a reserved word (a
tacctl command, \c
.BR local ,
.BR all ),
and a generic name are refused. The host keys the device offers at its address
and ssh port are read with
.B "ssh\-keyscan \-T 5 \-p"
.I port
.B "\-t ed25519,ecdsa,rsa"
(with
.BR \-\-legacy\-ssh ,
.B ssh\-rsa
is asked for by name too, and when
.B ssh\-keyscan
reads no key, tacctl's own ssh client reads them, stopping at the host key
without sending a password) and pinned. Their
.B SHA256:
fingerprints are printed with the command that shows them on the device's
console; compare them before the first connection. With
.BI "\-\-host\-key SHA256:" fingerprint
the device is registered only when it offers a key with that fingerprint, and
only that key is pinned (the other keys it offers are listed, unpinned). With
.B \-\-no\-host\-key
nothing is scanned: the device is registered unpinned and carries a
.B hostkey-unpinned
notice. A device that does not answer the scan, or offers no key matching
.BR \-\-host\-key ,
is refused and nothing is written. The output says which scope, if any, answers
the address.
.IP
Next to the scan,
.B add
reads the device's SNMP
.B sysName.0
(see
.BR "config snmp" )
and prints it
.RI ( "The device calls itself '" sysname "' (SNMP sysName)." ).
When it is neither the name given, the
.BR \-\-hostname ,
nor the first label of either (compared without regard to case), a warning
names the fixes and the add goes ahead. No answer within the
timeout (one retry), an empty sysName, or no SNMP set up gives one info line
.RI ( "No SNMP answer from " address "; no name hint." );
when a scan recorded a NAS-Identifier for the address, that is shown instead,
labelled as such.
Nothing of the name is stored.
.B \-\-no\-lookup
skips the lookup. With no
.IR name ,
the lowercased sysName is offered as the name and taken only after a
.B y
at a terminal; without a terminal or an answer, or when the sysName is
not a valid device name or is a generic one (a WTI unit's Site ID
.BR WTI ),
the add is refused with the usage line. The offered name goes through every
other check of the add.
.IP
Unless
.B \-\-snmp\-location
or
.B \-\-no\-lookup
is given,
.B add
also reads the device's SNMP
.B sysLocation.0
(same credentials, timeout and retry) and stores a non-empty answer as the
device's location
.RI ( "Location: " text " (read from the device)" ).
A value the registry does not accept is shown with the reason and not stored. A
device that reports none gets one line
.RI ( "Location not set: tacctl device location " name " '<text>'" ),
and at a terminal the add offers to enter one (blank skips). The location never
makes the add fail.
.IP
.B \-\-pull
then reads the device's configuration as
.B device config pull
does, for this one device, as you (the same password prompt, the same report
after the add's own output). Nothing is pulled unless
.B \-\-pull
is given. It is refused before anything is registered when the tier may not run
.BR "device config pull" ,
when the add is run as root, or when the vendor is not cisco, juniper or wti. A
device no scope's prefixes cover is registered and not pulled: one line gives
.B scope prefixes
.IR scope " add " cidr
and the pull command. A pull that fails leaves the device registered and the
command exits with the pull's status.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command device add
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBengineer\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-vendor\fR \fIcisco|juniper|wti|other\fR
The vendor (default other).
.TP
\fB\-\-hostname\fR \fI<dns>\fR
The device's DNS name.
.TP
\fB\-\-port\fR \fI<n>\fR
Its ssh port (default 22).
.TP
\fB\-\-description\fR \fI<text>\fR
A description of the device.
.TP
\fB\-\-snmp\-location\fR \fI<text>\fR
Its place (the SNMP location the walkthroughs render).
.TP
\fB\-\-legacy\-ssh\fR
SHA\-1 key exchange, CBC ciphers and ssh\-rsa; the configuration syntax is \-\-config\-syntax.
.TP
\fB\-\-config\-syntax\fR \fI<syntax>\fR
(cisco) auto, ios12 or modern: the configuration syntax (default auto: the pull's version line says).
.TP
\fB\-\-host\-key\fR \fISHA256:<fp>\fR
Register only if the device offers this key; pin it alone.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-host\-key\fR
Register without a pinned key (a hostkey\-unpinned notice).
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-lookup\fR
Do not read the device's own name and location by SNMP.
.TP
\fB\-\-pull\fR
After registering, read its configuration as 'device config pull' does (as you; needs a vendor and a scope).
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-generic\fR
(add, rename, import) Allow a generic name such as 'switch'.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command device add
.TP
.BI "device onboard " "address " "\-\-login " "local-user " "[ \-\-name " name " ] [ \-\-host\-key SHA256:" fingerprint " ] [ \-\-legacy\-ssh ] [ \-\-check ] [ \-\-section " list " ] [ \-\-json ]"
Superusers, Junos devices: bring a blank device, reached with one of its local
accounts, under tacctl in one run. Any local account may be named, a
break-glass account included; only
.B root
is handled apart (its csh wrapper). In order:
.RS
.IP 1. 4
Identity: the host keys the address offers are read and shown with their
fingerprints, and one question is asked before any login,
.RI "Trust these keys and send the local password of '" user "' to " address "? [y/N]" ;
.BI "\-\-host\-key SHA256:" fingerprint
answers it with the key read on the device's console. Without a terminal and
without
.B \-\-host\-key
the run is refused (exit 2), and without a terminal only
.B \-\-check
runs.
.IP 2. 4
Pre-flight as the local account (its password asked once on the terminal,
never kept or cached):
.BR "show version" ,
no pending confirmed commit, a login class that may commit, no TACACS+ or
RADIUS server
.RB ( "system tacplus\-server" ,
.B radius\-server
or
.BR "accounting destination tacplus|radius server" ,
also in a group):
.IR "refused: the device already has a TACACS+ or RADIUS server; it is reached by the normal flow (device add, device config pull)" ,
and no local account of your own name (a login as you could be that
account's, not TACACS+).
.IP 3. 4
The name
.RB ( \-\-name ,
else the host name the device reports, else asked at the terminal when that
one is generic or taken) and the registration, through
.B "device add"
with the keys of step 1 pinned; the device's own
.B "snmp location"
and
.B "snmp description"
fill the registry's empty location and description (a set field is never
replaced). The device's host name is shown
.RI ( "device reports host name" )
and never fills the registry's hostname, which ssh would dial instead of the
address. A device an earlier onboard registered, and that was never
read as your own login, is taken again under its name and pinned keys; any
other registered address is refused with the normal flow named.
.IP 4. 4
The pull as the local account: the record says
.BR login=local: \fIaccount\fR.
.IP 5. 4
The first-time plan of the ordinary planner and the device's own
.B "commit check"
as the local account, as
.B "device config apply \-\-check"
does it;
.B \-\-check
stops here (registered, nothing committed). The plan never holds the
break-glass accounts, root, the local account itself, system services or
interfaces.
.IP 6. 4
The review: the change with every secret elided, answered by typing the
device's name back; then your own password (your login, TACACS+; the password
cache may answer it).
.IP 7. 4
The commit as the local account under
.B "commit confirmed"
.RB ( "config devices apply\-confirm\-timeout" );
every session after it is your own TACACS+ login: a fresh login, the landed
change, the reads of the planned sections; then the confirm in the held local
session. The fresh login's
.B "show cli authorization"
must name the template user your group's Juniper class renders as its current
user, with that class; a local account (your own name, logged in through the
local password) is not a proof. A fresh login that is refused or is not that
template user rolls the change back and leaves the local account as it was.
.IP 8. 4
The local account is never tried again, root included
.RI ( "was not probed" :
with
.B "authentication\-order tacplus"
a login would send its password to the TACACS+ server);
.B \-\-json
has
.B probe
with the value
.IR not\-probed .
.RE
.IP
.B root
logs in through the remote command of
.BR "\-\-login root" .
A handshake that fails on the algorithms is tried again with the legacy set
and the device is registered
.BR \-\-legacy\-ssh .
Refused before anything is sent:
.B \-\-login
naming your own login,
.B \-\-section
without
.B aaa
(exit 2), an address no scope answers, a break-glass hold on it (release it
first), a registered device whose pull records cannot be read, and, unless
.BR \-\-check ,
a caller who is not an active member of that scope or whose group has no
Juniper class. What the device reports is shown with its control characters
replaced.
.B \-\-json
prints one object at the end (the progress goes to standard error). Logged:
.B "auth.notice device onboard user= device= approved=1 shape= login="
at the answer and
.B "auth.info device onboard user= device= addr= login=local:<account> result="
at the end, never a password. Exit status: 0 applied, checked or no change; 1
refused, failed, rolled back or pending; 2 wrong arguments; 130 interrupted.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command device onboard
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-login\fR \fI<local\-user>\fR
The device's local account to log in as (any, a break\-glass one included; root through its CLI wrapper): its password is asked once on the terminal and never kept or cached.
.TP
\fB\-\-name\fR \fI<text>\fR
The registry name (default: the name the device reports; a generic or taken one is asked for).
.TP
\fB\-\-host\-key\fR \fISHA256:<fp>\fR
Trust only this key, read on the device's console (no question; needed without a terminal); pin it alone.
.TP
\fB\-\-legacy\-ssh\fR
SHA\-1 key exchange, CBC ciphers and ssh\-rsa (also chosen by itself when the handshake needs them); registered so.
.TP
\fB\-\-check\fR
(onboard) Register, read and check the first\-time plan on the device; commit nothing.
.TP
\fB\-\-section\fR \fI<list>\fR
Only these sections of the first\-time plan: aaa, roles, mgmt\-acl, snmp, system; aaa among them (the proof is a TACACS+ login).
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
One object at the end (the progress goes to standard error).
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command device onboard
.TP
.BI "device collect " "address... | \-\-discovered " "[ \-\-login " "local-user " "] [ \-\-host\-key SHA256:" fingerprint " ] [ \-\-register [ \-y ] ] [ \-\-json ]"
Superusers, Junos devices: read what an address nobody registered is, with one
login. For each address that no registry entry holds (a registered one is
refused before anything is sent, with the normal flow named; so is an address
no scope answers, one with break-glass held, and a caller who is not an active
member of the scope):
.RS
.IP 1. 4
The host keys it offers are read (nothing is authenticated) and shown with
their fingerprints, and one question is asked before any login,
.RI "Trust these keys and send " "the password of 'user'" " to " address "? [y/N]" ;
.BI "\-\-host\-key SHA256:" fingerprint
answers it for one address with the key read on the device's console. Without
a terminal and without
.B \-\-host\-key
the run is refused (exit 2).
.IP 2. 4
One login per address: yours (your tacctl password, which the password cache
may answer), or with
.B \-\-login
the device's local account, whose password is asked once on the terminal, never
kept or cached, and goes to each address once. The first refusal of the
password ends the run: the addresses after it are
.IR not-tried .
.IP 3. 4
In that one session:
.B "show version"
and the pull's read command, nothing else. An address whose
.B "show version"
is not a Junos answer is listed
.I not-junos
and its session closed; it is never registered.
.RE
.IP
The result is one table
.RB ( ADDRESS ", " HOSTNAME ", " MODEL ", " RELEASE ", " AUTH\-ORDER ", " SERVERS ,
.BR EN\-CLASS ", " NETCONF ", " LO0\-FILTER ", " LEGACY " and " RESULT ),
then the
.B "device add"
line each collected device would run (a device that reports no name the
registry accepts says so). Nothing is kept without
.BR \-\-register :
the read is discarded.
