a vendor
.BR other ,
and a device in no scope are left out and said so.
.PP
A WTI console server has no command that prints its configuration. It is read
as its menus show it, over ssh as you
.RB ( "\-\-transport auto" " and " ssh ;
.B "\-\-transport netconf"
is refused for a unit): the pages the tables of command
menu version 8.10 name (the login's help, the network status, the Site ID, and
under
.B /N
the SNMP access and trap pages, the TACACS+ and RADIUS pages and the IP tables
list) are opened in turn with only the keys a page lists. No prompt that takes
a value is opened, nothing is typed at a prompt, the prompts of the communities
and secret words are never visited, and the final ESC, which makes the unit save
its configuration, is never sent. The system name, contact and SNMP location
that the SNMP access page cuts at about 16 characters
.RB ( ... )
are read whole by opening the item's text prompt
.RB ( /N ,
25, then 4, 5 or 6) with a CR and leaving it with ESC, which keeps the value.
The user directory
.RB ( /F )
is read in a second connection with the same password. Both connections end on
a page, which writes nothing. Only the extraction is kept, not the pages; a
community the unit prints in clear is compared against what tacctl renders and
kept as a label. A record that holds a cut value is compared by its beginning,
with a note, until the unit is pulled again. The line of a unit says how many other sessions it had
.RB ( "1 other session on the unit" ;
a count, no name) and warns when its user directory could not be read (the
second connection was refused, or the list is longer than a pull reads): the
pull is
.B ok
all the same, and
.B breakglass
reads
.B n/a
with a note. Three results belong to a unit.
.B denied
is a login whose WTI level cannot configure it
.RB ( "the login's WTI level is User; SuperUser or Administrator is needed" ,
with
.B "tacctl group edit <group> wti\-level superuser"
for the login's group): no page is opened, the pull is a
failed one (exit 1) and the batch goes on.
.B unsupported
is a command menu that is not version 8.10
.RB ( "command menu version 7.4; tables are verified for 8.10" ),
checked before any page.
.B parse\-failed
is a page the tables do not know or that is laid out otherwise: the reason names
the page's path and its title line, never its text.
.PP
Junos is read over NETCONF (the ssh subsystem, a
.B <command>
RPC) where the device answers and over an ssh exec channel otherwise;
.B \-\-transport netconf
and
.B ssh
never fall back. IOS and IOS\-XE are read over the ssh command line, with
paging off, after
.BR "show version | include Version" ,
whose line is kept on the record with the configuration syntax it says (see
.BR "device config\-syntax" );
a device that answers no image line (the boot loader's
.B ROM:
and
.B BOOTLDR:
lines are none) or one that maps to no syntax, and has no setting, is refused
with the command to set one and nothing is recorded. A Cisco device is never
read over NETCONF:
.B "\-\-transport netconf"
refuses it before any login, as a failed line that leaves its
record alone, or as wrong arguments when every selected device is Cisco. From the
text six sections (and a seventh,
.BR system ,
for a scope that has a Junos system block; the four of a WTI unit, which are
.BR aaa ,
.BR mgmt\-acl ,
.B snmp
and
.BR breakglass )
are extracted, as in the table of
.BR "device config diff" ,
compared with what tacctl renders for that device and recorded
.RI ( /var/lib/tacctl/devices\-config.json ,
with the sections themselves in
.IR /var/lib/tacctl/device\-config/<name>.yaml ):
a secret is compared by presence and its value is neither kept nor printed. A
failed pull changes the record's result and leaves the sections of the last
good one.
.PP
A good pull also records three read-only facts of the device, replaced by every
good pull and never written to
.IR devices.yaml :
the release, the model and the serial number
.RB ( "device show"
and
.BR "device config list" ).
The release is the
.B Junos:
line of
.B "show version"
on Junos (else the
.B "set version"
statement of the configuration the pull read),
the version of the
.B "Cisco IOS Software ... Version"
line of the Cisco version output (else of its first image line), and a WTI
unit's command menu version. The model and the serial number are read with
more commands in the same session, after the configuration:
.B "show chassis hardware"
on Junos (the Chassis row's description and serial number; the Model line of
.B "show version"
when the row gave none) and
.B "show inventory"
on IOS and IOS\-XE (the first entry's PID and SN). A device that refuses a
command, or answers text that holds none, leaves the field empty: the pull does
not fail and its result is unchanged, and a command that stalls takes it at
most 15 seconds more. A WTI unit has a release only.
.PP
A Cisco device stores some typed lines in another form, which the comparison
takes as the typed ones: a standard ACL entry "permit host A" or
"permit A 0.0.0.0" is stored as "permit A"; trailing blanks and the order of
the aaa lines do not count; the "privilege exec level N <words>" lines IOS adds
for the words that lead to a command the rendering carries are not extra (when
nothing rendered is beneath one, it is extra, and so is the command it was
added for); the line an IOS 15.x or IOS-XE image keeps where a privilege line
is negated ("no privilege exec level 7 clear counters" leaves "privilege exec
level 15 clear counters") is the image's own and is neither compared nor
reported when the command is one tacctl lowers or has lowered (ping,
traceroute, monitor capture, clear counters, clear line, clear ip arp, clear
arp\-cache, clear mac address\-table dynamic, undebug all, show running\-config,
and the words leading to them), the level is that command's own, it has no
"all", and the rendering has no line for the command (a line at another level is
extra, and a rendered line the device lacks is still missing); an image that
does not know the last words of a privilege command (IOS 12.4 has no "monitor
capture") stores the ones it knows, which is not extra either (the command
itself stays missing, with a note that names the line the device holds
instead); and "snmp\-server queue\-length 100", which IOS 12.4 prints beside the
first community, is not extra when tacctl renders a community.
.PP
On Cisco the
.B aaa
section also holds the login method of the console and VTY lines
.RB ( "line con 0 > login authentication CONSOLE" ,
.BR "line vty 0 4 > login local" ),
read by range: with
.B "aaa new-model"
a line that has no
.B login
statement is on the default method list, which IOS does not print, and counts
as
.BR "login authentication default" ;
the rendered
.B "line vty 0 15"
is satisfied when the device's ranges with that method cover it, and a range
that says
.BR "login local" ,
.B login
or
.B "no login"
is a difference. A console with no explicit
.B "login authentication CONSOLE"
shows
.B "\- line con 0 > login authentication CONSOLE"
and
.BR "+ line con 0 > login authentication default" .
Without
.B "aaa new-model"
a line with no
.B login
statement has no method. The standard has no
.B "aaa authentication enable"
list, for TACACS+ and RADIUS alike: a
.B default
list of that kind other than
.B "aaa authentication enable default enable"
(what IOS does with no list) is an extra
.RB ( + ", severity ACCESS); a list of another"
name is an extra of that name. A source interface the device has
where the scope sets none is not listed. Legacy forms
.RB ( "tacacs-server host" " on a modern image, " "radius-server host"
beside a
.BR "radius server" " block, a server group of another name)"
are listed as extras
.RB ( + " lines), never as satisfied lines, and the walkthrough removes none of"
them:
.B "device config plan"
replaces an entry at the standard server's address in its region and
.B \-\-converge
removes the others; what neither does stays on the device until you remove
it there.
.PP
An old
.B "tacacs-server host"
entry at the standard server's address on a modern image, on a device that
.B plan
refuses (command authorization on and no server in use), is replaced from the
console, as a user that reaches privilege 15 there, in four steps:
.BR "no tacacs-server host " <address> ;
the standard's
.B "tacacs server TACACS"
block;
in each
.BR "aaa group server tacacs+ " <group>
that held
.BR "server " <address> ,
.B "server name TACACS"
and then
.BR "no server " <address> ;
then a new session and one command to check. When no group holds the old
address, because the method lists use
.BR "group tacacs+" ,
the standard group is created and pointed at the named block and the lists keep
using
.BR "group tacacs+" .
On a device that
also has
.B "aaa authorization console"
command authorization falls to local once the group has no server, and a
TACACS+ user is not in the local database: first, while the TACACS+ user's
commands still pass, type
.BR "configure terminal" ", " "no aaa authorization console" ", " end
(the standard never sets it and it is not put back); if the console session is
refused after it, type steps 1 to 4 from a new session as a user that reaches
privilege 15.
.PP
The presence of an
.B enable
secret or password is recorded, never its value: a device with none gets a
note under
.B aaa
when the rendering turns AAA on (with the server unreachable, a local user of
privilege 15 is then the only way to level 15). A record that predates these
checks is not compared on the lines (a note says so) and gets no note about the
secret until the next pull.
.PP
One line per device (rewritten in place on a terminal, appended otherwise):
.BR ok ,
.BR "ok, differs in aaa,snmp" ,
.B "failed: <reason>"
(authentication failed, timed out, unreachable, a host key that is not the
pinned one, output that is not a configuration, for a WTI unit a login level
that cannot configure it or a command menu version that is not 8.10), then a
summary
.RB ( "12 ok, 2 differ, 1 failed" ).
At most
.B device.config.max_concurrency
devices are read at once (8 by default, at most 64;
.B \-\-concurrency
lowers it for a run and is refused above it, naming
.BR "config devices" ),
each for at most
.B \-\-timeout
seconds (10 to 600, default
.BR device.config.timeout ,
connect included). The first rejected password stops the batch (and the
password cache forgets it).
.B \-\-max\-failures
stops starting devices after that many failures. The first Ctrl\-C stops
starting devices and lets the running ones finish (the rest are reported
.BR "not started" ),
a second cuts the running ones off; the status is 130 either way.
.B \-\-diff
prints the differences after the summary
.RB ( "ok (+N unmanaged)"
for a section that holds only statements of the device's own, as for
.BR "device config diff" );
.B \-\-json
prints one JSON object per device as it ends and a last object
.BR "{\(dqsummary\(dq: ...}" ,
the sections of a device with their
.B unmanaged
count when it is not 0.
.BR \-\-server " and " \-\-source
are those of
.BR "config cisco|juniper|wti" :
the addresses the expected configuration is rendered with, for devices behind a
translating firewall. A Cisco device registered with
.B legacy\-ssh
is compared with the IOS 12.x syntax. Each device is logged to syslog
.RB ( "auth.info device config pull user= device= transport= result= duration=" ),
and an engineer's pull logs the
.B secret\-read
lines the walkthrough logs. Exit status: 0 when every device was read
(differences are not failures), 1 when one failed or was refused, 2 for wrong
arguments, 130 when interrupted.
.IP
.BI "\-\-login " local-user
(superusers;
.BR pull ,
.B diff \-\-pull
and
.BR "plan \-\-pull" )
logs in to one device as one of its local accounts instead of you: the
device is named (no list,
.BR \-\-all ,
.BR \-\-scope ,
.B \-\-vendor
or
.BR \-\-stale :
exit 2), registered as a Junos device with a pinned host key, and a scope
answers its address; anything else is refused before a password is asked, and so is a device on
which break-glass is held (release it first). Any local account of the
device may be named, a break-glass account included; only
.B root
is handled apart. The
account's password is asked once on the terminal, sent to that one connection
and never kept: the password cache is neither asked, nor stored into, nor told
to forget. The record says
.BI login=local: account
and the pull is logged as
.BI "auth.info device config pull user=" "you " "device=" "name " "login=local:" account
(never the password).
.B root
is accepted only for a device that was never read as your own login (a pull
record file that cannot be read refuses it); its csh
is left at once by the remote command
