.TP
.BI "device config plan " "name[,name...] | \-\-all | \-\-scope " scope " | \-\-vendor " vendor " | \-\-stale [ \-\-pull [ \-\-login " local-user " ] ] [ \-\-section " list " ] [ \-\-converge ] [ \-\-secrets | \-\-no\-secrets ] [ \-\-plain | \-\-json | \-\-backout ] [ \-\-out " file " | \-\-out\-dir " dir " ] [ \-\-force ] [ \-\-from " address " ] [ \-\-restore\-point[=" fs "] ] [ \-\-exit\-code ]"
Print, for each device, what to paste to make it match what tacctl renders,
from its last pull. It reads no device unless
.B \-\-pull
reads them first and writes to none: the paste is the technician's. The output
of one device is, in this order: a box naming the device, its address, scope
and vendor; the
.BR report ,
one numbered item per finding
.RB ( missing ,
.BR different ,
.BR "out of order" ,
.BR extra ,
.B "secret not visible"
and
.BR "not plannable for this login" ),
each with its severity
.RB ( ACCESS :
anything on the login path, AAA, the authentication order, template users, the
management filter, a second AAA server;
.BR MONITORING :
SNMP communities and clients;
.BR COSMETIC :
order, contact, location, accounting events, privilege lines) and what the
paste does about it
.RB ( "Fixed by region line 3" ,
.BR "Left alone" ,
.BR "Not fixable by this login: EN\-CLASS cannot change 'snmp'" );
the line to type first, on the device, to check its name; and
.BR "one region" ,
between
.B "PASTE FROM HERE"
and
.BR "PASTE TO HERE" ,
which holds only statements the device takes as they stand, the wrapper lines
and one label comment per section. The region is safe pasted whole. On Junos
it is
.BR "configure exclusive" ,
the statements,
.BR "show | compare | no\-more" ,
.B "commit check"
and
.BR "commit confirmed N" :
unless the one word
.B commit
is typed by hand after the check, the switch undoes the paste by itself in N
minutes (N is
.BR "config devices confirm\-timeout" ,
default 5). The session holds the configuration lock until
.B exit
is typed (after
.BR commit ,
to confirm). If
.B "configure exclusive"
prints
.BR "error: configuration database modified" ,
nothing happened: another session has uncommitted changes, which that person
commits or discards before the paste is made again;
.B "error: configuration database locked by"
means another session holds the exclusive lock. On IOS it is
.BR "configure terminal" ,
the lines and
.BR end :
the change is live at
.B end
and nothing rolls it back by itself (there is no revert timer);
.B "write memory"
is typed by hand after the check, never in the region. After the region, as
comments: the check, the one line to type, how to undo, and what not to do
(type the follow-up before the check, paste outside the banners, paste into
another device, keep the output in a file when it holds a secret).
.PP
A statement in a standard hierarchy that the standard does not have is an
.BR extra :
the report gives the exact line that removes it, and it is left alone.
.B \-\-converge
makes a region that removes the extras of the kinds the standard names, for a
device that has nothing else to paste: make the plan after the paste and the
pull that verified it
.RB ( "device config pull <name> \-\-diff"
reads ok for
.BR aaa ,
and a new session as yourself works). That region is the whole output of the
device, between
.B "CLEANUP FROM HERE"
and
.BR "CLEANUP TO HERE" ,
never beside a first region, because whoever copies a whole output runs every
region in it. A device with a first region gets that region and one line,
.BR "cleanup comes after this region is pasted and verified: pull the device, then plan \-\-converge again" .
The cleanup removes other servers of the scope's own protocol and their groups
(IOS: a legacy
.B tacacs\-server host
line, a
.B tacacs server
block, an old
.BR "aaa group server" ;
Junos: another
.BR "system tacplus\-server" ,
or on a RADIUS scope another
.BR "system radius\-server" ),
a login class no user uses, and the entries of another client list. It never
removes an SNMP community (it and what is under it are left alone), a login, a
line, the enable secret, a method list (a named
.B "aaa authorization commands"
list included: the pull does not say which line uses it, and the report gives
the line to type), another ACL, a server or group of the other protocol (RADIUS
on a TACACS+ scope may serve 802.1X), a server that a staying
.B "system accounting destination <protocol> server <address>"
names, a server or group that a staying statement names, any server of the
protocol while a staying list uses
.B "group tacacs+"
or
.BR "group radius" ,
or a line that holds the address of
.BR \-\-from ;
without
.B \-\-from
it removes no client\-list entry. It is refused as a whole while the
standard's server is missing and the paste does not put it there, and a
section the pull's login could not see completely is not cleaned. On Junos the
report and the follow-up of the cleanup also ask that
.B "show system commit"
shows no pending
.B "commit confirmed"
(type the plan's
.B commit
first).
.PP
.B \-\-backout
prints the back\-out region alone
.RB ( "BACK\-OUT FROM HERE" ):
the statements that put back what the pull showed, for the sections the plan
changed (on IOS the
.B no
lines in reverse order with the console and VTY login leaves typed again,
never a
.BR "no line" ;
a secret tacctl does not hold is a comment saying to type it again). Every
plan that has changes keeps its back\-out beside the device's record
.RI ( /var/lib/tacctl/device\-config/ <name> .backout ;
0600, stamped with the pull it was computed from, the device's address and the
sections it undoes, a secret's value never in it, carried by
.B "device config forget"
and
.BR "device rename" ).
.B \-\-backout
prints the kept one whenever one exists, with a first comment line that names
the sections and the pull, and prints it even when the plan itself cannot be
made (a stale record), with one line on standard error. A narrower plan from
the same pull does not replace it; a later plan with changes does, and the
report then says
.BR "this plan replaces the kept back\-out of the plan from the pull of <time> (UTC); print it first with 'tacctl device config plan <name> \-\-backout'" ;
a plan with changes and no line tacctl can type to undo them removes it and
says so; a plan with nothing to paste leaves it. For a device whose address
changed since the plan it says
.B "nothing to back out: the kept back\-out is for another device address"
(exit 0); with none kept it is computed from the pull, and with no plan to
undo it says
.B "nothing to back out: no plan was made from an earlier pull of <name>"
(exit 0).
.B "\-\-backout \-\-pull"
is refused (exit 2).
.B \-\-out\-dir
also writes it beside the plan.
.PP
.BR \-\-restore\-point [ =\fIfs\fR ]
(IOS and IOS\-XE only) adds a restore point: a block of its own before the
region, outside the
.B "PASTE FROM HERE"
banners, between
.B "RESTORE POINT FROM HERE: <device>"
and
.BR "RESTORE POINT TO HERE: <device>" .
It is one
.B "copy running\-config <fs>tacctl\-<device>\-<yyyymmdd\-hhmm>.cfg"
and one empty line (it answers the
.B "Destination filename"
prompt; a paste tool that drops blank lines turns the closing banner into the
file name, and
.B "file prompt quiet"
turns the prompt off), where
.I <device>
is the device's name up to its first dot and the time is the plan's, in UTC.
Paste the block first and on its own at the privileged prompt, then check that
.B "dir <fs>"
lists the file (for
.BR "archive config" ,
that
.B "show archive"
lists a new entry) before you paste the plan; the copy needs free space on
<fs>. The file holds the device's secrets in their own forms: protect it and
delete it when done.
<fs> is the file system named
.RB ( \-\-restore\-point=disk0: :
lower case letters and digits, then a colon; a network file system such as
.B tftp:
is refused) or, for the bare flag, the device's own archive path from the
pull's record (the file system word only); the block is then the one line
.BR "archive config" .
A bare flag for a device whose last pull read no archive path is refused
(exit 2) and says to name a file system or to pull again. The back\-out gains
an alternative after its
.B no
lines, as comments:
.B "configure replace <file> force"
(after
.BR "archive config" ,
the newest entry of
.BR "show archive" ),
with the caveats that where a release lacks it
.B "copy <file> running\-config"
only merges, that on IOS 12.4
.B "configure replace"
may skip the removal of
.B "aaa new\-model"
or stop at a line it cannot remove (an
.B "ntp server"
with options)
(do not rely on it for AAA; use the
.B no
lines), and that the file and any copy the replace leaves are to be
deleted afterwards. The kept back\-out records the file (its first comment
line says
.BR "its restore point is <file>" ).
The flag is refused for a device of another vendor, with
.B \-\-plain
and with
.BR \-\-backout .
The report says that the
.B "archive config"
form has not been checked on a device.
.PP
On a Cisco device the region follows the order of the standard block, and
.BR "command authorization is held back" :
the
.B "aaa authorization commands"
and
.B config\-commands
lines are in the paste only when the pulled configuration already uses the
scope's server (its address in a server block or host line and a login or
exec list that names the group). Once the commands list names the server every
further command, the back\-out's included, is asked of it, and it answers FAIL
for a user it does not know and never falls through: a device whose TACACS+
path has not been proven could end with no session able to run a command. The report then says that command authorization
is not in this paste, to check
.B "show privilege"
in a new session with your own account and plan again: the first plan puts
everything else, the second the one group of lines left. A
.B default
list of
.B "aaa authentication enable"
on the device (the standard has none:
.B enable
is the local enable secret) is removed with its own
.B no
line after the added lines; the back\-out types it again, and a list of
another name is left alone. A Cisco device that has no local user at privilege
15 and no
.B "aaa new\-model"
is refused for
.B aaa
(add the break\-glass user first). IOS silently refuses a second server entry
with the same address and key, so a device that holds the standard's server as
a named
.B "tacacs server <OTHER>"
block or as the legacy
.B tacacs\-server host
line on a modern image cannot take
.B "tacacs server TACACS"
beside it. The region then replaces it in an order that keeps the pasting
session: the command\-authorization lists that name the group are taken off
first, then the old entry and block (or
.BR "no tacacs\-server host" ),
then the standard block and the rest of the standard's order, and the lists
again last. A group of another name that held the old entry is pointed at the
standard block by its own name. The back\-out is the inverse (the old block and
entry back, its key a note for you to type, the lists last) and starts by
taking command authorization off. A
.B "radius server <OTHER>"
block at the standard's address and ports is replaced the same way. Only a device
that authorizes commands and shows no server in use (no login or exec list
names a group) is refused for
.B aaa
with the steps by hand below the region, because nothing proves that the
pasting session is a TACACS+ user. The old entry is removed by the region only
when the plan types the standard block's address or key. The syntax is the
setting of
.B "device config\-syntax"
or the pull's version line, never the ssh flag; a device that needs a pull is
not planned. A Junos statement that a group applies is never changed: its
section is refused.
.PP
A WTI console server has nothing to paste: its settings are the items of
numbered menu pages, a value is live as soon as its Enter is accepted, the
configuration is written to flash when a top\-level menu is left with ESC, and
the unit has no commit\-confirm and no rollback. Its plan is
.BR "a checklist of menu steps for you to type" ,
between
.B "DO THESE STEPS ON: <unit>"
and
.BR "END OF STEPS" ,
one comment line for each setting that differs: the keys typed from the
prompt
.RB ( "/N 28 4" ),
the item's label, what to enter after the arrow (an address item asks the
protocol first:
.BR "1 (IPv4), then 192.0.2.10" )
and, after
.BR "was:" ,
the value the pull showed. The order keeps the way in open: Multiple Logins
first
.RB ( "/N 31" ,
rendered On: a unit with it Off refuses the second session that the checks and
the pull log in from), then the SNMP page, then the page of the scope's
protocol (its Enable last), then
.B "ESC ESC"
(the save) and a
.B CHECK
step: a login as a member of the scope from a second session and
.BR /H .
Above the steps the report says what to have ready: the version check
.RB ( /H
must show
.BR "Version 8.10" ;
.B /J
shows the Site ID the pull read),
a serial console or a second session that stays open, and
.B /SN
to see that no other administrator is on the unit.
A secret step names the command that shows the
value
.RB ( "<the scope's TACACS+ secret: tacctl scope secret show lab>" ,
.BR "tacctl scope snmp lab show \-\-reveal" ):
a unit's plan types no secret unless
.B \-\-secrets
is given;
.B \-\-no\-secrets
is the default made explicit. A secret the unit has is compared by presence
only; a community, which the unit prints in clear, is compared with the
rendered one and its value is never in a record, a diff or
.BR \-\-json .
.PP
The IP tables list is the unit's firewall and can lock out the way in, so it
is presented and never applied: between
.B "IP TABLES (replaces every entry; see the notes)"
and
.B "END OF IP TABLES"
the output has one
.B <iptables>
block, the whole rendered list followed by the unit's own tail as the pull
showed it (the final DROP, the OUTPUT ACCEPT and the three policies; none when
the pull showed none, and the plan warns that the list restricts nothing until
the walkthrough's last step, Step 11), for the page's prompt at
.B "/N, 5"
to replace every entry. The lines of that block are the only lines of a unit's
output that are not comments. A list that differs in an entry, an extra entry
or the order is one finding. The section is refused, with the reason in the
report and the rest of the plan still made, when the list would hold more than
48 entries (the tail counted), holds an entry the paste does not take (not
printable ASCII, not
.B "iptables \-A"
or
.BR "\-P" ,
longer than 256 characters), does not permit the tacctl server's address to
reach the unit's ssh, or does not permit the address given with
.BR \-\-from ;
without
.B \-\-from
a list that ends with the final DROP, and the pull's other sessions, are
warnings. A rule for tcp 22, a port range that holds 22, every tcp port or
every protocol permits ssh. A plain entry of the permit list renders as a rule
for every protocol and port, and a list that pastes one carries a WARNING line
at the top of the report
.RB ( "N plain permit(s) of the rendered list allow every protocol and port" ).
A scope with no management permit list (or none that permits ssh) has no IP
tables section, and one whose server address is not known is refused for it.
What the standard does not render and the unit has is its own and is left
alone (the SNMP system name and trap settings, the tail, the other protocol's
page while it is switched off, the RADIUS items the walkthrough leaves alone);
a second host, a read/write community, the other protocol's page switched on
and a local account the scope does not record are findings, listed with the
step that removes them
.RB ( "/N 25 8" ", then " "<SPACE><CR>" ).
.B breakglass
is compared and reported, never planned (walkthrough Step 10). A step on the
AAA page is warned about: a wrong secret word, host, port or service name does
not stop a login but returns every login to the unit's Default User Access
level.
.PP
For a unit
.B \-\-backout
prints the steps again with the values the pull showed as their targets, last
step first, a secret or community as a line that says to re\-enter the one you
keep, and the old IP tables list as a
.B <iptables>
block between
.B "BACK\-OUT, IP TABLES"
and
.B "END OF IP TABLES"
(first, because it is the change that can lock out; a list that was empty is
cleared slot by slot with
.BR <SPACE><CR> ),
under the same WARNING line when the list it puts back holds plain entries.
.B \-\-converge
offers only the removal of a read/write community
.RB ( "/N 25 8" )
and the Enable of the protocol the scope does not use set to Off, for a unit
with nothing else to type.
.B \-\-plain
and a
.B \-\-section
of
.B roles
or
.B system
are refused for a unit (exit 2).
