.TP
.BI "device config apply " "name[,name...] | \-\-all | \-\-scope " scope " | \-\-vendor juniper | \-\-stale [ \-\-check ] [ \-\-confirmed ] [ \-\-max " n " ] [ \-\-pause " seconds " ] [ \-\-hold ] [ \-\-no\-canary ] [ \-\-section " list " ] [ \-\-transport " auto | cli | netconf " ] [ \-\-concurrency " n " ] [ \-\-timeout " seconds " ] [ \-\-max\-failures " n " ] [ \-\-server " address " ] [ \-\-source " address " ] [ \-\-exit\-code ] [ \-\-json ] [ \-y ]"
Write the plan to Junos devices: what
.B "device config plan"
would have a person paste, typed by tacctl, under a confirmed commit and
proven. It logs in as you, as
.B pull
does (the password is asked for once and never stored), and has two phases.
The
.BR check :
each selected device is read, planned from that read, loaded in an exclusive
configure session
.RB ( "configure exclusive" ),
checked with its own
.B "commit check"
and discarded; the devices that need a change are then shown grouped by
.BR shape
and by what the review shows of each (the same change under one heading,
every secret elided, a statement whose per\-device value differs printed for
each device after its name, with what the plan keeps and what it leaves out;
two devices of one shape whose warnings, sections left out, kept statements
or not\-found deletes differ are two headings) and ONE question is asked,
answered by typing the device's name back (one device) or the number of
devices (several). A device whose plan refuses a section (what makes
.B "device config plan"
exit 1) is not applied: its row is
.BR "failed: the plan refuses <section> (<why>)" . It
needs a terminal; with none the only way through is
.B "\-\-confirmed \-\-yes".
The
.BR apply :
each device is committed under
.B "commit confirmed N"
.RB ( "config devices apply\-confirm\-timeout" ),
proven by a fresh login as you through the device's own login path, by
.B "show configuration | compare rollback 1"
being the change the check showed and by a read of the planned sections
lacking nothing, and then confirmed in the session that committed, with a
minute of the window left. A proof that fails cancels the commit
.RB ( "rollback 1" ,
.BR commit )
and the device is
.BR "rolled back" ;
a device whose state could not be read after the confirmed commit is
.BR "PENDING until <time>" :
over the command line it undoes the change at the end of its window, not when
tacctl's session dies, and any
.B commit
or
.B "commit check"
typed on it confirms the change, so nothing may be typed on it before then.
The next apply or check of it reads its commit history first and records what
happened; while the window stands nothing is sent. Where the history alone
does not say (a commit after tacctl's by
.B "root via other"
is the device's own rollback or a commit of the system, or the commit after
it is someone else's), the device's own rollbacks do: with tacctl's commit at
history entry N,
.B "show configuration | compare rollback N"
empty is
.B applied
and
.B "compare rollback N+1"
empty is
.BR rolled\-back ,
both read in one operational session; anything else is
.B unknown
and never applied, whatever the rendering is now. The settled result is
printed above the device's line
.RB ( "the earlier apply <id> by <user> was rolled back by the device at <time>" ;
JSON
.BR settled )
and logged
.RB ( "auth.notice device config apply settle user= device= id= result=" ).
The record says
.B pending
from the moment the confirmed commit is typed, so a tacctl killed during the
verification leaves a record the next run settles; a record that cannot be
written then is said at once, in red, on standard error, and the transaction
goes on.
.PP
A device is reached over NETCONF or the command line, as
.B \-\-transport
(default
.BR device.apply.transport )
says.
.B auto
uses NETCONF for a device whose last read found the NETCONF service answering
and whose login may commit, and otherwise the command line, with the reason
after the device's line
.RB ( "via cli: NETCONF port closed at the last read" ;
a device never read says nothing; JSON
.BR via ,
.BR via_reason );
.B cli
is the command line always; and
.B netconf
never falls back: a device that does not answer or cannot confirm is
.BR failed .
Over NETCONF the change is loaded under the candidate's lock (a Junos device
refuses a confirmed commit in a private session), checked, committed with a
confirmed commit and confirmed in the session that committed; a session that
closes or dies without confirming leaves the commit pending until its window
ends, as over the command line, and a failed verification is cancelled at once
(\fBrollback 1\fR and a commit) while tacctl runs. A
candidate that holds another editor's staged change is refused.
A change to the NETCONF service itself
.RB ( "scope netconf" ,
.BR "device netconf" )
is always made over the command line, because its verification depends on the
service; with
.B "\-\-transport netconf"
it is refused. Its verification is a NETCONF probe on a fresh login before the
confirming commit: enabling the service must be answered by a hello as you,
disabling it must find the hello no longer answered
.RB ( "port closed"
or
.BR "no hello" );
a probe that cannot tell fails the proof and the change is rolled back. The
line says
.B "[NETCONF verified: hello ok]"
or
.BR "[NETCONF verified: not answering]" ,
and the record's NETCONF probe becomes what was proved.
.PP
.B \-\-check
stops after the device's own check: nothing is committed, the shape and the
result are recorded
.RB ( \-\-exit\-code :
exit 2 when a device needs a change).
.B \-\-confirmed
applies only shapes an earlier apply confirmed and skips the others
.RB ( "unknown shape <hash>: review and apply it by hand first" ),
and is the one form
.B \-y
answers the review for;
.B \-y
without it is refused (exit 2).
.B \-\-max
(default 5) caps the devices of every apply run. The first device of every shape is
applied alone and the rest of its shape follow only when it applied
.RB ( \-\-no\-canary :
together);
.B \-\-pause
(default
.BR "config devices apply\-pause" )
waits between devices and
.B \-\-concurrency
applies several at a time (default one; the checks run in the pool of
.BR pull ).
.B \-\-max\-failures
defaults to 1. At the start of each device's apply the break\-glass hold, the
registry entry (name, address, vendor, hostname, port, host keys) and the
scope that covers it are read again: a device held, changed or no longer
yours since the review fails with the reason and nothing is sent to it.
.B \-\-hold
keeps the change only when, after the fresh login, you log in with one
account of each class and type
.BR OK ;
anything else, or no answer while a minute of the window is left, rolls it
back, and a line typed before a device's question is not its answer. It needs
a terminal and is refused with
.B \-\-json
(exit 2).
.B \-\-section
limits the sections
.RB ( aaa ,
.BR roles ,
.BR mgmt\-acl ,
.BR snmp ,
.BR system ).
.B \-\-json
prints one object per device and phase, then the summary, and sends the review
to standard error.
.PP
Never written, whatever is given:
.BR breakglass ,
.B netconf
(unless the scope's or the device's NETCONF setting enables or disables the
service: then
.BR "set system services netconf ssh"
with its limits, or
.BR "delete system services netconf" ),
.BR "system root\-authentication" ,
.BR "system services" ,
.BR interfaces ,
groups, a local user's credentials, the root user, the account that runs it and
its login class, and the deletion of a whole hierarchy tacctl renders; every
statement is held against the planner's ownership tables before the first is
typed, and a statement outside them stops the device with nothing sent. A
statement that a group supplies is never changed. Refused before any login: a
caller below the superuser, a device held for break\-glass, one no scope covers
or whose scope is not yours; a Cisco device or a WTI unit named is a failed
row and a selector leaves them out. A device that reports another name than
its registered name or hostname fails
.RB ( "calls itself" ),
another editor's staged or open configuration fails it
.RB ( "other editors or staged changes" ).
.PP
The first Ctrl\-C starts no more devices and lets the running one finish, the
second cancels: before the commit the candidate is discarded, after it the
commit is cancelled where the window allows and otherwise left PENDING. Exit
status: 0 every device applied, was checked or had no change; 1 a device
failed, was rolled back or is pending, the review was not answered, or devices
were selected and none was applied or found already applied
.RB ( "\-\-confirmed \-\-yes"
that skipped them all:
.BR "Nothing was applied: every device was skipped." ,
JSON
.BR "\(dqstop\(dq:\(dqnothing\-applied\(dq" );
2 wrong arguments; 130 interrupted. The last check or apply of a device is kept in its
record (when, by whom, the change identifier, result, shape, sections,
counts and the confirming commit: no raw output and no secret) and shown in the
.B APPLIED
column of
.BR "device config list" ,
the Configuration row of
.B "device show"
and their JSON as
.BR applied .
Every device is logged
.RB ( "auth.info device config apply user= device= id= shape= result= duration=" ,
.B "auth.notice device config apply user= approved= shapes= canary="
for the answer, the canary being the first device of each shape or
.B none
with
.BR \-\-no\-canary ,
and
.B "auth.notice device config apply user= device= id= shape= sections= result= commit= until="
for each apply and
.B "auth.notice device config apply settle user= device= id= result="
for an earlier apply's pending record settled, never a value).
