.SS SSH to a device \fB(tacctl ssh)\fR
.TP
.BI "ssh " name|address " [ \-p " port " ] [ \-X | \-Y ] [ \-g ] [ { \-L | \-R | \-D } " spec " ]... [ \-\- " "ssh args" " ]"
Open an ssh session to a registered device or an enrolled host, named by its
name or its registered address. tacctl resolves the name as root and admits
only an active tacctl user (in the store, not disabled, with a password) whose
scopes include the entry's, at every tier, superusers included; an entry in no
configured scope is refused to everyone, and a local account that is not a
user of tacctl is refused
.RI ( "'user' is not a tacctl user" ;
.IR "'user' has no access to scope 'scope' (device name)" ).
Each refusal is logged to syslog
.RB ( "ssh DENY user=" "\fIuser\fR " device= "\fIname\fR " scope= "\fIscope\fR " reason= "\fIreason\fR" ,
auth.warning). An admitted session is logged as
.BI "ssh user=" user " device=" name " addr=" address
(auth.info; a
.BI from= client
field names where the caller logged in from, when it can be told: the first field of
.B SSH_CONNECTION
or the host of the terminal's login record) and, when ssh ends, as
.BI "ssh end user=" user " device=" name " status=" n " duration=" seconds\fR;
.BR ssh (1)
runs as the invoking user
.RB ( "sudo \-u " "\fIuser\fR " \-H ),
with the terminal, returning its exit status. The login is always the invoking
user's own name and the session authenticates by password only: no agent, no
key, no other login; an enrolled host's enrolment account and identity are
never used. It never runs ssh as root: run by root itself (no
.BR SUDO_USER ,
or
.BR SUDO_USER=root )
it refuses with
.IR "tacctl ssh runs ssh as the user who invoked it; run it from your own account, not as root" .
It needs a terminal on stdin
.RI ( "a terminal is required" ).
An address that is not registered is refused with the
.B "device add"
command to register it.
.IP
ssh connects to the device's hostname, else its address (an enrolled host: its
target's host), with its port unless
.B \-p
is given, and always
.B "\-o ConnectTimeout=10 \-o PubkeyAuthentication=no \-o PreferredAuthentications=keyboard\-interactive,password"
.RB ( wti :
.BR "PreferredAuthentications=password" ).
A device with
.B legacy\-ssh
gets
\fB\-o KexAlgorithms=\:+diffie\-hellman\-group14\-sha1,\:diffie\-hellman\-group1\-sha1
\-o Ciphers=\:+aes128\-cbc,\:aes192\-cbc,\:aes256\-cbc,\:3des\-cbc
\-o HostKeyAlgorithms=\:+ssh\-rsa \-o PubkeyAcceptedAlgorithms=\:+ssh\-rsa\fR
(old IOS; OpenSSH 8.5 or later; the CBC ciphers are appended after the strong
ones). A device with pinned host keys adds
\fB\-o UserKnownHostsFile=\:/var/lib/tacctl/\:ssh/known_hosts
\-o GlobalKnownHostsFile=none \-o StrictHostKeyChecking=yes
\-o HostKeyAlias=\fR\fIname\fR\fB \-o UpdateHostKeys=no\fR,
so ssh accepts the pinned key and nothing else. An unpinned device is checked
against the user's own known_hosts, after its
.B hostkey\-unpinned
notice is printed (unless acknowledged). Arguments after
.B \-\-
follow the target, so ssh takes them as the remote command; they cannot
replace an option.
.IP
.BR \-X " and " \-Y
forward X11 (untrusted, trusted) to this server's display
.RB ( DISPLAY ,
which sudo keeps for tacctl; without one the command says to log in with
.BR "ssh \-X" );
.BR \-L ,
.B \-R
and
.B \-D
(each repeatable) are ssh's local, remote and dynamic port forwardings, with a
.I spec
of addresses, ports, host names or socket paths only. The session's log line
then carries
.BR forward= \fIkinds\fR
.RB ( x11 ", " local ", " remote ", " dynamic ).
In the login console they are for the tiers of
.B "console forwarding tiers"
only (default
.BR superuser );
any other tier is refused
.RI ( "Forwarding (\-X, \-Y, \-L, \-R, \-D) is not available to the tier tier in the console" ;
logged as
.BR "ssh DENY ... reason=forward" ).
There,
.B \-L
and
.B \-D
listen on loopback only:
.B \-g
and a bind address other than loopback
.RB ( 0.0.0.0:8443:localhost:443 ", " *:1080 ,
or an empty one) are refused
.RB ( "ssh DENY ... reason=gateway" )
unless
.B "console forwarding gateway\-ports"
is enabled.
.B \-g
lets
.B \-L
and
.B \-D
ports listen on every address, as ssh's
.BR \-g .
.IP
From a session of the login console (the console marks each line with
.BR TACCTL_CONSOLE= \fIsession\fR),
ssh reads no configuration file and opens nothing but the session: it starts
with
.B "\-F /dev/null \-o PermitLocalCommand=no \-o ControlMaster=no \-o ClearAllForwardings=yes \-o ForwardAgent=no"
.RB ( ClearAllForwardings
left out when the session forwards) and
.B "\-o EscapeChar=none"
(unless
.BR "console ssh\-escape enable" ),
the target follows
.B \-\-
so no word after it is an option, and words after the name's
.B \-\-
that start with
.B \-
are refused. An entry with no pinned host key is refused
.RI ( "'name' has no pinned host key, so the console does not connect to it; an administrator pins it: tacctl device hostkey name accept" ;
logged with
.BR reason=unpinned ).
Its log lines end with
.BI console= session\fR.
.IP
When ssh ends with status 255 on a pinned device, tacctl reads the device's
keys again; if the pinned key is not among them it prints the pinned and the
offered fingerprints, the console command that shows the key on the device,
and
.BI "tacctl device hostkey " name " accept"
or
.BI "set SHA256:" fingerprint
to re-pin after verifying. The read-only tier may run it (the tiers sudoers
rules grant
.B "tacctl ssh *"
and
.BR "tacctl device ssh *" ).
.IP
When the session's password cache holds the user's password
.RB ( "console password\-cache" )
and the device has pinned host keys, ssh is not asked for it on the terminal:
it is started as the user directly, without
.BR sudo ,
with the helper
.B SSH_ASKPASS
(the tacctl binary; it answers a password prompt and nothing else),
.BR SSH_ASKPASS_REQUIRE=force ,
.BR "\-o NumberOfPasswordPrompts=1" ,
.BR "\-F /dev/null"
and the pinned host key checked strictly; the log line then ends
.BR password=cached .
Words after
.B \-\-
that start with a dash, an unpinned device, and an account that is not in the
local account database keep the prompt. An ssh that ends with status 255
makes the cache forget the password.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command ssh
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBreadonly\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-p\fR \fI<port>\fR
Connect to <port> (default: the device's port, else 22).
.TP
\fB\-X\fR
Forward X11 to this server's display (untrusted, as ssh \-X).
.TP
\fB\-Y\fR
Forward X11 to this server's display (trusted, as ssh \-Y).
.TP
\fB\-g\fR
Let \-L and \-D ports listen on every address, as ssh \-g.
.TP
\fB\-L\fR \fI<spec>\fR
Forward a local port, as ssh \-L (repeatable).
.TP
\fB\-R\fR \fI<spec>\fR
Forward a remote port, as ssh \-R (repeatable).
.TP
\fB\-D\fR \fI<spec>\fR
Open a SOCKS proxy, as ssh \-D (repeatable).
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command ssh
.SS Break-glass hold \fB(tacctl breakglass)\fR
A device's local accounts
.RB ( "tacctl scope breakglass" )
reach its vty only while no server answers it: a server's refusal is
final, so a local login there is refused while the server is up. On a Cisco
console the
.B CONSOLE
list is local, so a local account logs in there with the server up, lands at
privilege 1, and
.B enable
with the local enable secret takes it to 15. A
.I hold
makes the server stop answering the registered devices (or enrolled Linux
hosts, below) named on purpose, so
that their local accounts can be used on a vty (and those of a Junos or WTI
device) while the server is up. A hold is for an exceptional case; it has no
timer, no automatic release and no whole\-scope form. Several devices are
held in one command, best effort: each is checked on its own, the ones that
pass are held together (one write of
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
one render, one restart of each backend that changed) and the others are
named with why under
.BR "Not held:" ;
the exit code is 1 when any was not held.
.PP
An enrolled Linux host
.RB ( "host list" )
is held the same way, by name, with the same record and carve\-out. Its
address is the one
.B "host enroll"
or
.B "host sync"
recorded, else its ssh target's when that is an address (a name is never
resolved for this). The server then answers no TACACS+ or RADIUS login and no
sudo check for it, while the host's local administrator (an account that does
not authenticate through tacctl) and its console work. The report, the prompt
and the way in are the host's own, and the audit lines read
.B host=
in place of
.BR device= .
This server's own host
.RB ( "host enroll \-\-local" ),
a loopback address, a listener's address, a host with no recorded address and
no address as its ssh target, and a name that is both an enrolled host and a
registered device (refused as ambiguous, both named) are not held; a device is
not held, nor moved by
.BR "device address" ,
at an enrolled host's address, and a host is not held at a registered
device's address (a hold there would silence the device too). A host is held
by its name: an address that is only its ssh target's is refused, naming the
host. While the hold stands
.BR "host sync" ,
.BR "host show \-\-check" ,
.B "host enroll"
of the registered host
.RB ( "host move" ),
.B "host target"
with a change,
.B "host provisioner rotate"
and
.B "host unenroll"
are refused with the hold named, before anything is attempted over ssh;
.B "host sync \-\-all"
skips the host;
.B "host list"
marks it,
.B "host show"
has a
.B Break\-glass
section, and
.B "tacctl ssh"
warns and connects.
.PP
The record is
.B breakglass_hold.<device>.address|since|by|reason
in
.IR tacctl.yaml
(a device name's dots are written
.B ~
in the key), so every snapshot holds it. Both renderers take the held address
out of every prefix of every scope, and out of every tagged address (RADIUS):
a /24 that holds it answers as the eight prefixes /25 to /32 that
do not, a prefix equal to the address is gone, and every other address is
answered by the scope that answered it before.
.I store.yaml
is never changed.
.B tacquito.yaml
and
.B clients.conf
carry a comment that names the address, the device and the prefixes it was
carved from, never the reason. Each backend whose file changed restarts once,
at the hold and at the release; a release that changes nothing restarts nothing.
Only the address the device is registered at is carved: a device that sends its
AAA from another address (a source interface, a NAT) is still answered. A record holds a device only while the device is
registered at the record's address (a host: while a host of the name is
enrolled and its recorded address is the record's); any other record (the
device was removed by hand, or registered under the name at another address) is an
orphan, which
.BR "breakglass list" ,
.B status
and
.B "config validate"
name, whose address stays carved, and which
.B "breakglass release"
removes.
While a hold stands,
.B "device remove"
of the device is refused,
.B "device address"
moves the record to the new address (the registry and the record in one write,
one render, one restart of each backend that changed; the new address passes
the checks a new hold passes, and one question names both addresses unless
.B \-y
is given),
.B "device rename"
moves the record,
.BR "device config pull" ,
.B "diff \-\-pull"
and
.B "plan \-\-pull"
refuse the device (a batch skips it),
.B "device config plan"
of the last pull names the hold before its items and the cover page marks the
device,
.B "tacctl ssh"
warns and connects, and
.BR "tacctl rollback"
is refused.
.B "backup restore"
is a render like any other: a hold comes back with a snapshot that has it and
goes with one that lacks it, and the restore names the devices held now and
after it when that set changes.
.B status
(a red line per hold),
.BR "device show" ,
.BR "device list" ,
.BR "device check" ,
.BR "device scan" ,
.BR "scope routing" ,
.BR "scope show" ,
.BR "scope list" ,
.B "scope lookup"
and
.B "config validate"
say so for as long as it stands. Both verbs write an
.B auth.info
line per device (the reason is in the hold's:
.BI "breakglass hold device=" name " addr=" ip " reason=\(dq" text "\(dq by=" user
and
.BI "breakglass release device=" name " addr=" ip " held=" duration " by=" user )
and take the snapshot every change takes. A hold that
.B "device address"
moves writes
.BI "breakglass hold\-move device=" name " from=" ip " to=" ip " by=" user .
.TP
.BI "breakglass hold " "name[=reason][,name[=reason]...]" " [\-\-reason " text ]
Hold break\-glass on registered devices and enrolled Linux hosts, given as one comma
list in one argument (quote it when a reason holds a space). Each device has a reason, 1 to 256
characters: its own after
.B =
(which ends at the next comma, so a reason that holds a comma is given with
.BR \-\-reason )
or the
.B \-\-reason
default; a name with neither is a usage error that names it, and a name is
given once. Each name is checked on its own, as below: the ones that pass
are held together, and each that does not is named with why on a
.B "Not held: <name>: <why>"
line, in the confirmation and again in the report. When none passes nothing is
asked or changed and the verb exits 1; when some were not held it exits 1 after
holding the rest. A hold made by another superuser while the names were typed
is skipped and named the same way. At a terminal it asks for the names of the
devices that passed typed back as printed, comma separated (for one device,
the device's name; there is no
.BR \-\-yes ;
without a terminal the verb refuses) and prints one report: the record of each
device, what was rendered and which backends restarted (said once), the way in
for the vendors present (for one device
the way in of its vendor; Cisco:
ssh as a local account, the local
.B CONSOLE
login and
.B enable
with the local secret; Junos: a local
.BR "system login user" ;
WTI: its Fallback Local), that the device's accounting goes to a server that
does not answer it,
.B "Held now:"
(every hold that stands) and
.B "When done:"
(the release of the devices held).
A
.B "Carved:"
line says that only the registered address is carved and that a device that
sends its AAA from another address is still answered, and a
.B "Warning:"
line adds, when the seen data hold no request from the registered address, how
to learn the address the device sends from
.RB ( "tacctl device config pull" ", " "show tacacs" ,
the device's log). A backend whose restart failed is reported as
.BR "NOT restarted" ,
with the command, and the verb exits 1; the record and the rendered files are
kept. A device already held is refused.
Refused, with nothing changed, for a name that is neither a registered device
nor an enrolled host, or is both (ambiguous), is this server's own host, has no
address, is held already, is at a loopback address or an address a listener of
either backend binds (the server's own logins come from 127.0.0.1), is a
device at the address of an enrolled host (a hold there would silence the host
too), or that no scope covers (it is unanswered already). Superuser only.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command breakglass hold
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.PP
Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-reason\fR \fI<value>\fR
(hold) Why, 1 to 256 characters; kept with the record and in the log (the reason of every name that has no <name>=<reason>).
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command breakglass hold
.TP
.BI "breakglass release " "name[,name]..."
Make the server answer the device or host again. Works from the record alone: a
device removed from
.I devices.yaml
by hand is still released, and an entry that carved nothing (no address, or one
that is not an address) is removed by name.
A restart that failed is reported as
.B "NOT restarted"
and the verb exits 1. The report names how long it was held, by whom and why,
what was rendered, the check
.RB ( "tacctl device config pull " device " \-\-diff" ,
then a new ssh login as yourself) and the holds that remain. Superuser only.
Given a comma list, the devices that are held (or whose entry carved nothing)
are released together, in one write, one render and one restart of each
backend that changed, with one report block each and the rendering and the
check said once; a name that is not held is named on a
.B "Not released: <name>"
line, the rest are released, and the exit code is 1. When none is held nothing
is changed.
