Show or set the lifetime of a cached password from the moment it was cached, in
hours (1 to 24, default 8). Use does not extend it.
.TP
.B console forget
Forgets the cached password of this
.B "tacctl shell"
or console session now. It is a word of the shell and the console, handled in
the session's own process without sudo, and works while the cache is off (it
says so). Run as a command, outside a session, it only says where the cache is.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command console forget
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBoperator\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command console forget
.TP
.BI "console system\-shell tiers " "[ " csv "|none ]"
The tiers that may start their system shell from the console (default
.BR superuser ).
The same tiers may open an editor from the console
.RB ( "scope block ... edit|adopt" ,
.BR "device block ... edit|adopt" ):
an editor can start a shell.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command console system-shell
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command console system-shell
.TP
.BI "console system\-shell path " "[ " path " ]"
The system shell, default
.IR /bin/bash .
It must be an absolute path to an executable file listed in
.IR /etc/shells .
.TP
.B console install
Puts the server's pieces in place, as
.B host sync
of the host enrolled with
.B \-\-local
does: the line
.I /usr/local/bin/tacctl\-console
in
.IR /etc/shells ,
and sshd's drop-in
.I /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/tacctl\-console.conf
for the members of
.BR tac\-console :
.B "ForceCommand /usr/local/bin/tacctl\-console"
(every login, remote command and subsystem, sftp included, reaches the
console, which reads the client's command from
.BR SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND ),
.B "DisableForwarding yes"
(left out, with
.BR "AllowAgentForwarding yes" ,
when
.B console agent\-forwarding
is enabled; for the tiers of
.B "console forwarding tiers"
a block before it sets
.BR "DisableForwarding no" ,
.B "AllowTcpForwarding yes"
and
.BR "X11Forwarding yes" ),
.BR "AllowTcpForwarding no" ,
.BR "AllowStreamLocalForwarding no" ,
.BR "X11Forwarding no" ,
.BR "PermitTunnel no" ,
.BR "PermitTTY yes" ,
.B "PubkeyAuthentication no"
(a key login would bypass TACACS+),
.B ClientAliveInterval 300
and
.BR "ClientAliveCountMax 2" .
Each change of the drop-in is checked with
.BR "sshd \-t" ;
when sshd refuses it the previous file comes back and the command exits 1,
otherwise sshd is reloaded
.RB ( "systemctl reload ssh.service" ,
or
.BR sshd.service ).
Each piece is reported
.BR Installed: ,
.B Updated:
or
.BR Unchanged: ,
then the check below runs. The engineer tier's own drop-in
.I /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/00\-tacctl\-engineer.conf
(every forwarding and tunnel closed for the members of
.BR tac\-engineer )
is written the same way, and by every
.B host sync
of the server even when the console is not installed; an earlier
.I tacctl\-engineer.conf
is removed. Refused when
.I /usr/local/bin/tacctl\-console
is missing
.RB ( "tacctl upgrade" " makes it)."
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command console install
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command console install
.TP
.B console remove
Takes the console's drop-in (checked with
.BR "sshd \-t" ,
sshd reloaded) and the
.I /etc/shells
line away; the engineer tier's drop-in stays (an earlier
.I tacctl\-engineer.conf
is renamed). Refused, nothing changed, while an account still has the console
as its login shell; they are named, with the way to give them
.I /bin/bash
back.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command console remove
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBsuperuser\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command console remove
.TP
.B console check
Whether sshd applies the console's settings: the drop-in present and this
release's, the
.B Include
of
.I sshd_config.d/*.conf
in
.IR /etc/ssh/sshd_config ,
and
.B "sshd \-T"
for the first account whose shell is the console. It also checks the
engineers (the drop-in of the engineer tier and that its name sorts before
the console's, a leftover
.IR tacctl\-engineer.conf ,
each member's shell) and names every member of
.B tac\-superuser
that is a tacctl account and not a superuser-tier tacctl user, a removed
user's account included (its UID is in tacctl's range; root is not named). A
member that is no tacctl account (a local administrator added by hand) is only
listed, and does not make the check fail. Exit 1, with the red
warning, when anything is off.
.PP
The console asks tacctl for its settings with the hidden
.BR _console\-policy ,
which the tiers sudoers rules grant to every tier.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: command console check
.RS
.PP
Requires: \fBoperator\fR.
.RE
.\" END GENERATED: command console check
.SS Console sessions \fB(tacctl\-console)\fR
.I /usr/local/bin/tacctl\-console
is a symbolic link to the tacctl binary; started under that name (as a login
shell,
.BR \-tacctl\-console )
tacctl is the console: the interactive shell
.RB ( "tacctl shell" )
with the prompt
.IB host "> "
(the server's short host name) and the banner
.IR "tacctl\~console on host \(em type 'help'. Devices: device list. This session is logged." ,
whose lines run as
.PP
.RS
.B sudo
.RB [ \-n ]
.BI TACCTL_CONSOLE= session
.B /usr/local/bin/tacctl
.I words
.RE
.PP
with the rule of
.B tacctl shell
for
.BR \-n .
The login environment is discarded except
.BR TERM ,
.BR LANG ,
.BR LC_* ,
.BR HOME ,
.BR USER ,
.BR LOGNAME ,
.BR SSH_CONNECTION ,
.B SSH_CLIENT
and
.BR SSH_TTY ;
.B PATH
is
.I /usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
and
.B SHELL
the console. At the start it asks the root side for its settings
.RB ( "sudo \-n tacctl _console\-policy" );
without an answer the defaults apply (30 minutes idle, no system shell), and
only the read-only commands are listed. The answer also carries the caller's
tier, which Tab,
.BR ? ,
a typed space and
.B help
use to list only the commands that tier can run (see
.BR "tacctl shell" ).
The session ends after the idle timeout at the prompt
.RB ( "console idle\-timeout" ;
not while a line runs).
.PP
.BI "\-c " line
(the remote command sshd passes:
.BR "ssh " "\fIserver\fR " "'user list'" )
runs one tacctl line, with the connection as its standard input. A string
that is not one line beginning with a tacctl command or
.B help
\(em
.BR scp ,
.BR sftp ,
.BR rsync ,
any program, the shell's own words, an empty string \(em is refused with
.I "the tacctl\~console does not run programs; file transfer is not available"
and exit status 126, and nothing runs. Any other argument is refused the same
way
.RI ( "the tacctl\~console takes no options" ).
With standard input not a terminal and no
.BR \-c ,
the lines of the input run as a batch, as in
.BR "tacctl shell" .
.PP
.B system\-shell
(the console's own word; interactive sessions only) starts the user's system
shell
.RB ( "console system\-shell path" ,
default
.IR /bin/bash )
as the user, without arguments, with the console's environment and
.BR SHELL= \fIpath\fR;
the console's idle timer does not run meanwhile, and sshd's drop-in still
governs the connection. It is open to the tiers
.B "console system\-shell tiers"
names (default: superusers only); otherwise it is refused with
.IR "system-shell is not available for the tier tier on this console. An administrator enables it with: tacctl console system-shell tiers ..."
(status 1). In a batch or through
.B \-c
it is refused with status 126.
.PP
Syslog (tag
.BR tacctl\-console ):
.BI "console start session=" id " user=" user " from=" address " tty=" tty " mode=" interactive|command|batch\fR,
.BI "console end session=" id " user=" user " reason=" exit|eof|idle|hangup|signal|command|failed " lines=" n " status=" n\fR,
.BI "console DENY session=" id " user=" user " reason=command first=" word
(auth.warning),
.BI "console system\-shell start" \fR,
.B end
.RB ( status= ", " duration= )
and
.BR DENY .
Each line is also in sudo's log, and
.B tacctl ssh
logs
.BI console= id\fR.
.SH RADIUS DEVICE CONFIGURATIONS
.BR "config cisco" ,
.B "config juniper"
and
.B "config wti"
render the configuration for logging in to a device against the RADIUS
backend when given
.BR "--protocol radius" ,
or without the flag for a scope that resolves to RADIUS (its auth-method,
else its only protocol;
.BR "scope auth-method" ).
The server address, the authentication and accounting ports (the
.B auth
and
.B acct
listeners shown by
.BR "config listen --backend radius show" )
and the scope's shared secret are filled in; the management ACL, the
exec timeout, the scope's
.B aaa-order
(the server first, or local first) and the local fallback behave as in
the TACACS+ output. Without the flag, and for a scope that does not
resolve to RADIUS, the output is the TACACS+ configuration.
.PP
Cisco output is a
.B "radius server"
block,
.B "aaa group server radius"
(named by
.BR "scope radius-group" ),
.BR "aaa authentication login" ,
.BR "aaa authorization exec"
(the privilege level comes from the reply's
.BR "Cisco-AVPair shell:priv-lvl=N" ,
which the scope must send: see
.BR "scope vendor-attrs" )
and
.BR "aaa accounting exec" ,
with the same
.B "privilege exec level"
mappings. Juniper output is
.BR "system radius-server" ,
.BR authentication-order ,
.B "system accounting destination radius"
and the template users that the reply's
.B Juniper-Local-User-Name
selects, with the per-class allow-commands and deny-commands rules,
which stay in force because the class is local to the device.
.PP
RADIUS has no per-command authorization and no command accounting, and
tacctl's RADIUS accepts PAP only. Each RADIUS output lists under the
configuration what the Access-Accept carries for the device
.RB ( Service-Type
and the vendor's attribute, enabled for the scope or for tagged
addresses only) and the TACACS+ features that are not available
(rules set with
.B "group commands"
are not enforced by the server).
.PP
tacctl refuses, with an error and no output, when the configuration
would not work: the RADIUS backend is not enabled
.RB ( "backend enable radius" ),
the scope's
.B "scope protocols"
filter leaves out
.BR radius ,
the scope sends the vendor's attribute to none of its devices (neither
enabled with
.B "scope vendor-attrs"
nor an address tagged with
.BR "scope devices" ;
the error prints the command that enables it),
or the scope secret holds a character that IOS or Junos read as syntax
(whitespace, quotes, a backtick, a non-ASCII character, or any of
.BR "? ! # $ \\e ; { } [ ] | & < > , * ( )" ).
Letters, digits and
.B ". _ + / = : @ % ^ ~ -"
paste unquoted, and
.B "scope secret generate"
makes such a secret. The secret is shared with TACACS+, so changing it
means changing it on every device of the scope. A secret longer than 63
characters renders with a warning. A listener bound to one IPv4 address
supplies the server address; an IPv6 or loopback bind is warned about.
Operator overrides
.IR cisco-radius.template ,
.I juniper-radius.template
and
.I wti-radius.template
in the templates directory work as for the TACACS+ templates; see
.BR FILES .
.SH TIERS
A user of tacctl who has a local account on the tacctl server (a member of
.BR tac\-users )
reaches tacctl through
.BR sudo (8)
and is held to a tier: by tacctl itself, which checks the caller before every
command, and by the rules of
.BR "config sudoers tiers" ,
which are written from the same table. A group's tier is the one set on it
.RB ( "group edit " \fIgroup\fR " tier " \fItier\fR ,
kept as
.BI tier. group
in
.IR tacctl.yaml ),
else the band of its priv-lvl: 15 and up
.BR superuser ,
7 to 14
.BR operator ,
below 7
.BR readonly .
The fourth tier,
.BR engineer ,
sits between operator and superuser and is only ever set on a group. A user
that is unknown or disabled, or whose group has no priv-lvl, has no tier and
may run nothing. A group at priv-lvl 15 or more that has no tier setting and is
not the built-in
.B superuser
group holds its members at the operator tier until a
superuser who is not a member sets it again; so does a
.I tacctl.yaml
that cannot be read, for every managed caller. Root, and a local administrator
who is no tacctl user, are not held to a tier.
.PP
A tier may run what the tiers below it may. Under each tier below are the verbs
for which it is the lowest; everything not listed (users, groups, scopes and
the change of any secret, backends, backups and their restore, upgrades and
every global setting) is the superuser's. The shell and the console list only
the verbs the caller's tier can run. Each entry of COMMANDS names its tier on a
.B Requires
line.
.\" BEGIN GENERATED: tiers
.TP
.B readonly
(no arguments), passwd, status, version, help, \-h, \-\-help,
user list, user show, group list, group show, scope list,
backend list, backend status, ssh, device list, device show,
device notices, device ssh, device ssh\-config, _completion\-names,
_console\-policy, \-\-version, \-v, hash
.TP
.B operator
log tail, log search, log failures, log accounting, config validate,
backup list, device check, device scan, device discover,
device export, device config, console show, console check,
console forget
.TP
.B engineer
device add, device remove, device rename, device address,
device hostname, device vendor, device port, device description,
device location, device legacy\-ssh, device config\-syntax,
device hostkey, device import, scope devices, device config,
config cisco, config juniper, config wti, host list, host show,
scope staging, scope secret, scope snmp, device snmp, scope block,
device block, scope show, breakglass list
.TP
.B superuser
Every verb not listed above, and every verb of the tiers below.
.PP
The gate sees two words, so a verb that the code splits further is listed at its
two\-word level and then limited by the code:
.TP
.B breakglass list
An engineer lists the holds of the devices of their own scopes only; hold and release need the superuser tier.
.TP
.B config cisco
An engineer may print the walkthrough and read source\-interface for a scope of their own; setting or unsetting source\-interface needs the superuser tier.
.TP
.B device block
An engineer may read the configuration blocks of a device of their own scope (list, show [\-\-effective] [\-\-reveal], check); set, remove, edit and adopt need the superuser tier.
.TP
.B device config
An operator may run list; show, pull, diff and plan need the engineer tier (a device's walkthrough carries its scope's secret, a plan can type it, and a pull logs in to the device as you), and an engineer gets the devices of their own scopes only; forget, apply and shapes (a check or an apply writes to the device; shapes files what an apply confirmed) need the superuser tier.
.TP
.B device import
An engineer imports from standard input only (device import \-).
.TP
.B device list
The CONFIG column (and config, release, model and serial of \-\-json) is the operator tier's; a read\-only user's list has none.
.TP
.B device show
The Configuration, Release, Model and Serial rows (and config, release, model and serial of \-\-json) are the operator tier's; a read\-only user's output has none.
.TP
.B device snmp
An engineer may read the settings of a device of their own scope (show [\-\-reveal], version, port, timeout, clients list); every setter and clear needs the superuser tier.
.TP
.B host show
An engineer reads what tacctl recorded of a host; \-\-check logs in to it and needs the superuser tier.
.TP
.B scope block
An engineer may read the configuration blocks of a scope of their own (list, show [\-\-reveal], check); set, remove, edit and adopt need the superuser tier.
.TP
.B scope secret
An engineer may run show for a scope of their own; set and generate need the superuser tier.
.TP
.B scope snmp
An engineer may read the settings of a scope of their own (show [\-\-reveal], version, port, timeout, contact, clients list); every setter, clear and test needs the superuser tier.
.TP
.B scope staging
An engineer may run list for a scope of their own; the other verbs need the superuser tier.
.\" END GENERATED: tiers
.PP
An engineer's verbs reach only the devices, hosts and secrets of the scopes
the engineer is a member of; another scope's device is not found, and a change
that would touch one is refused. Each read of a secret by an engineer is
logged to syslog (never the value).
.SH CONVENTIONS
The following patterns apply uniformly across subcommand families.
.TP
.B No arguments
Dispatcher commands
.RB ( user ,
.BR group ,
.BR config ,
.BR log ,
.BR backup ,
.BR hash ,
and every nested dispatcher such as
.BR "scope prefixes" ,
.BR "scope secret" ,
.BR "scope aaa-order" ,
.BR "scope exec-timeout" ,
.BR "scope tacacs-group" ,
.BR "scope radius-group" ,
.BR "scope auth-method" ,
.BR "scope vendor-attrs" ,
.BR "scope devices" ,
.BR "scope mgmt-acl" ,
.BR "scope breakglass" ,
.BR "scope block" ,
.BR "user scope" ,
or
.BR "group privilege" )
print their own usage and return without side effects.
Scalar getter/setters
.RB ( "config loglevel" ,
.BR "config listen" ,
.BR "config metrics" ,
.BR "config password-age" ,
.BR "config bcrypt-cost" ,
.BR "config password-min-length" ,
.BR "config secret-min-length" ,
.BR "config branch" ,
.BR "scope default" )
print the current value when called with no arguments.
.TP
.B Multi-item input
Every
.B add
/
.B remove
subcommand that takes a CIDR or a Cisco exec command accepts either a
single value or a comma-separated list
.RI ( "cidr1,cidr2,..." ).
Every input is validated first; a bad entry aborts the operation
without writing anything.
.TP
.B CIDR semantics
Every CIDR-list subcommand
.RB ( "scope prefixes" ,
.BR "config allow" ,
.BR "config deny" ,
.BR "config mgmt-acl" )
canonicalizes input before storage:
.B 10.1.5.5/24
becomes
.BR 10.1.5.0/24 ;
.B 2001:DB8::/32
becomes
.BR 2001:db8::/32 .
Exact duplicates (after canonicalization) are rejected as no-ops on
.BR add .
Overlapping CIDRs of different prefix lengths coexist
.RI ( "10.0.0.0/8 and 10.99.0.0/16 can both be present" ).
Stored order is by broadcast-address ascending (IPv4 before IPv6):
disjoint ranges sort by their end address, and an overlapping subnet
falls immediately above its containing supernet.
.TP
.B Scope prefix invariants
Every CIDR (after canonicalization) belongs to
.B exactly one
scope. Adding a prefix already owned by a different scope is rejected
with a message naming the owning scope; remove it
from the owning scope first with
.B "scope prefixes " \fIowner\fR " remove " \fIcidr\fR
before re-adding it elsewhere. Overlapping prefixes
.I across
scopes are allowed and routed to the narrowest scope that contains the client:
a scope with
.B 10.5.0.0/16
coexists with a scope holding the supernet
.BR 10.0.0.0/8 .
.PP
tacctl emits one
.B secrets:
entry per (scope, prefix) pair, sorted globally by prefix specificity
(v4 before v6; smaller broadcast first) after every mutation; a scope with N
prefixes becomes N entries that share
.BR name: " and " secret.key: .
.BR "scope list" ", " "scope show" " and " "config show"
present one entry per scope.
.PP
Do not hand-edit the
.B secrets:
block under multi-prefix scopes: the same-name entries must carry
identical
.B secret.key
values.
.B config validate
reports any divergence.
.TP
.B "clear and remove \-\-all"
.B clear
and
.B "remove \-\-all"
subcommands always prompt with
.B [y/N]
and print a warning about the resulting posture (for example, "no
clients can connect" or "fails open").
.TP
.B "Filters and opt-ins"
A setting that narrows something down is a filter: its verbs are
.B set
and
.BR clear ,
and empty (cleared) means everything
.RB ( "scope protocols" ,
.BR "config allow" ,
.BR "config deny" ).
A setting that turns something on is an opt-in: its verbs are
.B enable
and
.BR disable ,
and nothing is on until it is enabled
.RB ( "backend enable|disable" ,
.BR "scope vendor-attrs" ).
An opt-in has no
.BR set ,
.B clear
or
.BR none .
.TP
.B "Membership lists"
A list that names exactly what is in is a membership list, and empty
means nothing, not everything
.RB ( "user scope" ,
the scopes a user can authenticate from: none means nowhere;
.BR "scope prefixes" ,
the clients a scope serves: emptying it removes the scope).
Its verbs are
.B add
and
.BR remove :
.B "remove \-\-all"
empties it, and
.B "user scope"
replaces its whole list with
.BR replace .
.TP
.B Service restart
Changes to the store, and to the
.I tacctl.yaml
settings a backend renders (command rules, listeners,
.BR backends.* ),
re-render every enabled backend and restart the backends whose files
changed. Settings only device configurations read (mgmt-acl permits and
names, per-group priv-exec mappings, per-scope AAA order, exec timeout and
group labels) and tacctl's own tunables (bcrypt cost, password age, \&...)
restart nothing.
.TP
.B Prompts
Every confirmation prompt cancels, with a message, when standard input is
closed.
.B \-y
(or
.BR \-\-yes ,
.B \-\-force
where documented) answers yes.
.TP
.B Flags
Long-form flags
.RI ( --hash ,
.IR --scopes ,
.IR --scope ,
.IR --prefixes ,
.IR --secret ,
.IR --protocols ,
.IR --vendor-attrs ,
.IR --match ,
.IR --action ,
.IR --branch ,
.IR --protocol ,
.IR --method ,
.IR --backend ,
.IR --listener ,
.IR --server ,
.IR --name ,
.IR --port ,
.IR --identity ,
.IR --output )
take a single argument;
.IR --default ,
.IR --force ,
.IR --legacy ,
.IR --check ,
.IR --replace ,
.IR --json ,
.IR --local ,
.IR --all ,
.IR --build-on-host ,
.IR --allow-uid-mismatch ,
.IR --apply ,
.IR --hosts ,
.I --remove-home
and
.I -y
take none. Required positional arguments come before flags.
.SH BACKENDS
.TP
.B tacacs
TACACS+, served by tacquito built from source; installed by
.B "tacctl install"
and enabled by default.
.TP
.B radius
RADIUS, served by FreeRADIUS from the distribution's package; installed and
enabled by
.BR "tacctl backend enable radius" .
.PP
Every change renders the configuration of every enabled backend from the
store, all or none, and restarts only the backends whose files changed.
With more than one backend enabled,
.BR status ,
.BR "config show" ,
.B "config validate"
and
.B log
print a labelled section per backend
.RB ( "== Backend: radius (radius, freeradius) ==" ).
.PP
.B Listeners
are
.BI listeners. backend . name
in
.I tacctl.yaml
.RB ( "config listen" ).
The TACACS+ listener
.B default
(tcp :49) runs in
.BR tacquito.service ;
every further one is an instance
.BI tacquito@ name .service
of
.BR tacquito@.service ,
.B PartOf
and
.B WantedBy
.BR tacquito.service ,
so
.B "systemctl restart tacquito"
covers them all. Each unit has a rendered drop-in
.IR <unit>.d/tacctl.conf .
RADIUS listeners
.RB ( auth ,
udp :1812;
.BR acct ,
udp :1813) are
.B listen
sections of tacctl's FreeRADIUS configuration. TACACS+ over TLS is reserved
in the schema and not implemented.
.PP
.B RADIUS.
.B "backend enable radius"
installs
.B freeradius
and
.B freeradius\-utils
(Debian/Ubuntu; RHEL family from AppStream) and starts the distribution's
unit
.RB ( freeradius
or
.BR radiusd )
with a drop-in that runs tacctl's own instance
.RB ( "\-n tacctl\-radius \-D" " <its dictionary>" ):
the package's configuration is not read, edited or unlinked, so there is no
EAP, inner tunnel, proxying or default client. A unit that is already
running or enabled without tacctl's drop-in is somebody's RADIUS server and
is refused. One client per prefix of every scope RADIUS serves, with the
scope's secret; a user authenticates only from a client in one of their
scopes; disabled users and the
.B root
sink never do. PAP only, against the stored bcrypt hashes (no CHAP,
MS-CHAP or EAP). No per-command authorization and no command accounting.
An Access-Accept carries
.B Service-Type
always, and a vendor's privilege attribute only where the scope opts in
.RB ( "scope vendor-attrs" ", " "scope devices" ).
FreeRADIUS 3.2.5 and 3.0.27 log a "BlastRADIUS check" block for a client the
first time it sends Message-Authenticator and require it from that client
from then on.
.SH STORE AND GENERATED CONFIGURATION
.I /etc/tacctl/store.yaml
(users, groups, scopes, filters) and
.I /etc/tacctl/tacctl.yaml
(settings) are canonical. Generated from them:
.I /etc/tacquito/tacquito.yaml
and the tacquito unit drop-ins; for RADIUS
.IR tacctl\-radius.conf ,
.I tacctl\-radius.users
and
.I tacctl\-radius\-dictionary/dictionary
in the FreeRADIUS configuration directory.
.I /etc/tacctl/rendered.json
holds their checksums.
.PP
A change is checked against hand edits, snapshotted, written, rendered into
a staging area for every enabled backend and proved there (read back, the
daemon's own config check), then installed, and the backends whose files
changed are restarted; a failure at any step puts the store,
.I tacctl.yaml
and every generated file back.
.PP
.B Concurrent commands.
A command that changes the store or
.I tacctl.yaml
holds one lock,
.IR /etc/tacctl/.store\-apply.lock ,
from before it copies the files it may put back to after its last restart, so that a command that
fails does not put back what another one wrote in between. A second command
prints
.I "Waiting for another tacctl command that is changing the configuration..."
once and runs when the first is done;
.B Ctrl\-C
ends the wait (\c
.IR "Nothing was changed." ,
exit 1;
.B install
and
.BR upgrade ,
which have built or pulled by then, say that the store,
.I tacctl.yaml
and the services were not changed and to run the command again). Every write of
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
the store commands,
.BR "config render" ,
.BR "config listen|loglevel|metrics" ,
the RADIUS listener changes,
.BR "backend enable|disable" ,
.BR "store import" ,
.BR "store rollback" ,
.BR "backup restore" ,
.B upgrade
(after its self-update) and
.B install
(from its seed to its end) take it; a command that only reads does not. A file
that was edited by hand while a command ran is saved as
.I <file>.overwritten\-<timestamp>
in
.I /etc/tacctl
(and named in a warning) before the command puts its own copy back.
.PP
.B Drift.
A generated file that no longer matches its checksum was edited by hand:
.B status
and
.B "config validate"
show a
.B DRIFT
line and every change is refused (exit 3). Keep a hand edit of
.I tacquito.yaml
with
.B "store import \-\-replace"
then
.BR "config render \-\-force" ;
discard it with
.B "config render \-\-force"
(a copy goes to
.IR /etc/tacctl/backups/legacy/ ).
RADIUS files can only be re-rendered.
.PP
.B Legacy read-only mode.
Without a store (an upgrade whose gate stopped, or after
.BR "store rollback" )
tacctl reads
.I tacquito.yaml
directly: read commands work, commands that change users, groups, scopes
or filters are refused with
.IR "store not initialised" .
Only TACACS+ exists in that mode.
.SH UPGRADING
.B "tacctl upgrade"
pulls the clone in
.I /opt/tacctl
and, when the installed binary was not built from the commit now checked out,
builds it again and re-executes it once, so that
.B "tacctl version"
always matches the clone. Then, in this order, it:
.IP 1. 4
moves
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
.IR linux\-hosts ,
.IR linux\-uids ,
.I backups/
and
.I templates/
from
.I /etc/tacquito
to
.IR /etc/tacctl ,
leaving links at the old paths (idempotent; a regular file written there by
other software wins over the moved copy, which is kept under
.IR backups/legacy/ );
.IP 2. 4
installs
.B tacquito.service
and
.BR tacquito@.service ,
imports a hand-managed drop-in
.I tacctl\-overrides.conf
into
.I tacctl.yaml
once, renders
.I tacctl.conf
in its place and keeps the old file under
.IR backups/legacy/ ;
.IP 3. 4
on an install that has no store yet, runs the store gate:
.B "store import \-\-check"
(import, render, equivalence of what tacquito loads from both files, a load
test of the rendered file with the new binary on a loopback port). Only when
it passes is the store written, the old file kept as
.IR backups/legacy/tacquito.yaml.pre\-store. <timestamp>,
.I tacquito.yaml
rendered and tacquito restarted.
.PP
When the gate stops (unrepresentable content, a render that is not
equivalent, the tacquito binary missing) the upgrade still completes, leaves
.I tacquito.yaml
and the daemon as they were, and tacctl is in legacy read-only mode. Fix what
.B "store import \-\-check"
reports and upgrade again, or accept the difference:
.B "store import"
.RB [ \-\-force ],
then
.BR "config render \-\-force" .
.B "store rollback"
undoes the move into the store.
.PP
The shipped operator rules and privileges do not hold
.IR "clear ..." ,
.IR "monitor capture" ,
or the unbounded and interactive forms of
.B ping
and
.BR traceroute ,
and Junos
.B OP-CLASS
has no
.B clear
bit. A list stored in
.I tacctl.yaml
keeps what it holds, except an operator or readonly list equal to what 0.2.3
shipped, which the upgrade drops. The upgrade and
.B "config validate"
name the stored settings that still carry a removed command;
.B "group privilege reset operator"
and
.B "group commands reset <group>"
(or
.BR "group reset <group> --preset --only commands,junos" ,
for the values of a role preset of 0.2.3, which keeps its
.B show
denies)
are the way back. A device configured earlier keeps its old lines until they
are removed (until then its operators keep the old reach when the server does
not answer, and always over RADIUS). The first upgrade onto this release prints
them (on Cisco
.BR "no privilege exec all level 7 ping" ,
.BR traceroute ,
.BR "monitor capture" ,
and
.B "no privilege exec level 7 clear"
.IR "counters, line, ip arp, arp-cache, mac address-table dynamic" ;
on Junos
.BR "delete system login class OP-CLASS permissions clear" ),
and
.B "device config plan"
prints the lines a pulled device needs, in the form its image stores them.
.PP
The Cisco rendering has no
.B "aaa authentication enable"
line, so a pulled Cisco device that has the list reads
.B differs
in
.B "device config list"
until
.B "device config plan <device>"
is pasted; no new pull is needed. On a device that has the list the 0.2.5
walkthrough rendered, the plan is one line in its
.B aaa
region,
.BR "no aaa authentication enable default group " <G> " enable"
(the back\-out types it again); a list of another name stays, and a device
with no list has nothing to paste for it. Until the line is removed,
.B enable
on that device asks the server, and any user the server knows reaches
privilege 15 with their own password. A device with no enable secret has no
.B enable
once the list is gone: set one first (Step 0).
.PP
Going back to 0.2.9 is
.B "tacctl rollback 0.2.9"
(a dry run), then
.B "tacctl rollback 0.2.9 \-\-apply"
and
.BR "tacctl upgrade \-\-branch 0.2.9" ;
it removes the transport setting of
.B "device config apply"
and the NETCONF service setting of the scopes and devices, and lists what
0.2.10 left that 0.2.9 ignores (the
.B via
field of the apply records). Until 0.2.9 is installed, do not run
.BR "scope netconf" ,
.BR "device netconf" ,
.B "config devices apply\-transport"
or
.BR "device config apply \-\-transport" ;
run the rollback again if one ran. The snapshot it takes holds the 0.2.10 form
and is restored with 0.2.10. Going back to 0.2.8 is this rollback, the install
of 0.2.9 and then 0.2.9's own
.BR "tacctl rollback 0.2.8" .
.PP
Going back to 0.2.3 is
.B "tacctl rollback 0.2.3"
(a dry run), then
.B "tacctl rollback 0.2.3 \-\-apply \-\-yes"
and
.BR "tacctl upgrade \-\-branch 0.2.3" .
The rollback converts the keys and fields a 0.2.3 binary refuses (listed under
.B rollback
in COMMANDS).
Until 0.2.3 is installed, a command that writes the console's
settings, a device's SNMP settings or configuration syntax, a scope's Cisco
source interface or the
.B device.config
keys writes the 0.2.5 form again; run the rollback again to convert it. The
snapshot the rollback takes holds the 0.2.5 form and is restored with 0.2.5
(0.2.3's
.B "backup restore"
refuses a
.I tacctl.yaml
with a key it does not know). Going back to 0.2.2 is this rollback, the
install of 0.2.3 and then 0.2.3's own
.BR "tacctl rollback 0.2.2" .
.SH CONFIGURATION KEYS
.SS tacctl.yaml
.I /etc/tacctl/tacctl.yaml
holds the operator's overrides of tacctl's tunables (see
.BR FILES ).
A key set back to its default is removed from the file; a key that is not in
this list, or whose value does not fit, is refused with the reason before
anything is written. Read one key with
.BR "config get" ,
the merged result with
.BR "config show"
or
.BR "config dump" ,
and check a hand-edited file with
.BR "config validate" .
A name in angle brackets stands for a group, a scope, a backend or a listener;
the keys of a family exist once for each.
