.PP
A mapping
.B settings.password_cache
(\fBtiers\fR: a list of operator, engineer, superuser, default none;
\fBidle\fR: minutes, 1 to 120, default 15; \fBmax\fR: hours, 1 to 24, default
8) is written only when one of its three values is not the default. Set with
.BR "tacctl console password\-cache" .
.B "rollback 0.2.3"
takes it out of the file.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command did what it was asked, or showed what there was to show. A
confirmation answered no cancels the command with nothing changed, and ends
with 0 for the removals
.RB ( "user remove" ,
.BR "group remove" )
and for
.BR "store rollback" .
.TP
.B 1
The command refused or failed, and said why on standard error: a value that
does not fit, a name that does not exist or exists already, a store or
.I tacctl.yaml
that cannot be read or written, a caller whose tier does not allow the
command, a check that found a problem
.RB ( "config validate" ", " "console check" ", " "device check" ),
a device that could not be read, compared or planned
.RB ( "device config pull" ", " "device config diff" ", " "device config plan" ),
a device
.B "device config apply"
failed, rolled back or left pending (and a review that was not answered, and
an apply that applied none of its devices and found none already applied:
.B "\-\-confirmed \-\-yes"
that skipped them all), a step of a lifecycle phase that failed. An unknown
sub-command prints the
usage of its family and ends with 1 as well. Nothing was changed unless the
message says what was.
.TP
.B 2
The arguments were wrong for a verb that checks them itself and says so
.RB ( "store show" ,
.BR "store import" ,
.BR "store rollback" ),
or a backend id is unknown, or the backend does not provide the action asked
of it, or
.B "device config diff \-\-exit\-code"
found a device that differs, or
.B "device config plan \-\-exit\-code"
found a device that needs a change, or
.B "device config apply \-\-check \-\-exit\-code"
found a device that needs a change, or the arguments of
.BR "device config pull" ", " diff ", " plan ", " apply ", " shapes ", " list " or " forget
were wrong
.RB ( apply
without a terminal and without
.BR "\-\-confirmed \-\-yes" ,
and
.BR "apply \-\-hold \-\-json" ,
included).
.TP
.B 3
A backend command was refused because a file tacctl rendered holds a hand edit
that the command would discard (see
.BR "STORE AND GENERATED CONFIGURATION" ),
or
.B "store import \-\-check"
could not prove the import safe. Nothing was written.
.TP
.B 126
sudo could not be run to re-run tacctl as root, or
.B tacctl\-console
was given an argument it does not accept.
.TP
.B 127
sudo is not installed, and tacctl was not run as root.
.TP
.B 130
The command was interrupted at a prompt (Ctrl-C at a password prompt, for
example), or a
.BR "device config pull" ,
.B "device config diff \-\-pull"
or
.B "device config plan \-\-pull"
was stopped by Ctrl-C (the first stops starting devices and lets the running
ones finish, a second cuts them off), or a
.B "device config apply"
was (a transaction in flight finishes under the first; under the second a
candidate is discarded and a pending commit cancelled where the window allows,
else reported
.BR PENDING ).
A pull changes nothing on a device.
.PP
.B "tacctl ssh"
and
.B "tacctl device ssh"
end with the status of the ssh they run, and
.B "tacctl shell \-c"
with the status of the line it ran.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
tacctl reads the variables below. Everything else it needs is a fixed path
(see
.BR FILES ).
.TP
.B "SUDO_USER, SUDO_UID, SUDO_GID"
Set by
.BR sudo (8)
for the person behind it. tacctl takes the caller's name from
.BR SUDO_USER :
the tier gate, the target of
.BR passwd ,
the scopes an engineer reaches, the user
.B ssh
and
.B host
run ssh as. The pair
.B SUDO_USER
and
.B SUDO_UID
is checked against the passwd database before anything runs, and a
mismatch denies every command. Files a command writes for the caller
.RB ( "config linux script \-\-output" ,
for example) are handed to
.B SUDO_UID
and
.BR SUDO_GID .
.TP
.B HOME
Where the history of
.B "tacctl shell"
and of the login console is kept
.RI ( ~/.local/state/tacctl/history ).
.TP
.B "USER, LOGNAME"
The login name the console records in its session log
.RB ( USER
first, then
.BR LOGNAME ).
.TP
.B "SSH_CLIENT, SSH_TTY, SSH_CONNECTION"
Set by sshd. The console logs the client address and the terminal of a session;
.B tacctl shell
reads its lines from the pipe when a command arrives over ssh with
.B SSH_CONNECTION
set and no terminal
.RB ( SSH_TTY
unset).
.TP
.B SSH_AUTH_SOCK
The agent socket
.B host
and
.B ssh
hand to the ssh they run as the caller, so an agent's key reaches the host.
sudo drops it unless the tiers drop-in keeps it
.RB ( "config sudoers tiers" ).
.TP
.B DISPLAY
The X11 display of sshd's forwarding, handed to
.B "tacctl ssh \-X"
and
.B \-Y
after its shape is checked, and kept by the tiers drop-in for the console.
.TP
.B "VISUAL, EDITOR"
The editor of
.B "scope block ... edit|adopt"
and
.BR "device block ... edit|adopt" :
.BR VISUAL ,
else
.BR EDITOR ,
else
.BR editor ,
else
.BR vi ,
run as the invoking user. sudo drops both, so the sudoers drop-ins keep them for
those four command lines only; tacctl removes them from its own environment at
start, so no program it runs inherits them.
.TP
.B TACCTL_ASKPASS
.RI "Set by a " "tacctl shell" " or console session whose password cache is on, as " socket : token
in the environment of the
.B sudo
of each line that uses it, with a token made for that line and dead when it
ends (never on sudo's command line); the tiers drop-ins keep it for those
lines only
.RB ( "device onboard"
asks it for your own password after its review, and
.B "device collect"
for your own login; a line with
.B \-\-login
logs in as a device's local account and never has it). The root side of
.BR "device config pull" ,
.B "block ... adopt"
and
.B ssh
reads it once and removes it from its own environment, and the helper ssh runs as
.B SSH_ASKPASS
(the tacctl binary, started with
.B TACCTL_ASKPASS_HELPER=1
and the prompt as its only argument) answers a password prompt with it and
nothing else. See
.BR "console password\-cache" .
.TP
.B "TACCTL_COLOR, NO_COLOR, TERM"
Whether output is styled (bold titles, colour in the
.BR [INFO] ,
.B [WARN]
and
.B [ERROR]
lines and in help and listings). Styling appears only on a terminal, decided
for standard output and standard error separately: a stream is styled when it is
a terminal and
.B TERM
is neither empty nor
.BR dumb .
.B TACCTL_COLOR=always
styles both streams even into a pipe and
.B TACCTL_COLOR=never
neither; it wins over the rest, and any other value is ignored. Otherwise a
non-empty
.B NO_COLOR
means never. The tags carry their word, so nothing depends on colour. The
sudoers drop-ins keep
.B NO_COLOR
and
.B TACCTL_COLOR
so that the person's choice reaches the root process; without a drop-in the
variables do not cross
.BR sudo (8),
and the terminal alone decides. The login console decides by its terminal only:
its sessions carry neither variable.
.TP
.B TACCTL_CONSOLE
Set by the login console, as
.BI TACCTL_CONSOLE= session
on the command line of each sudo it runs, to mark a command as run from a
console session. The drop-in keeps it. tacctl reads it only to tighten what it
does (the hardened ssh, the
.B console=
field of the log lines).
.TP
.B TACQUITO_SRC
The clone of the tacquito source that
.B install
and
.B upgrade
build the server from (default
.IR /opt/tacquito\-src ).
.TP
.B TMPDIR
The directory where tacctl stages private copies while it works (rendered
configuration trees, a candidate sudoers file, a script for a host, a check of
an import), each made with mode 0600 or 0700 and removed on every path. Default:
the system temporary directory
.RI ( /tmp ).
.BR sudo (8)
may replace the variable with its own environment policy, in which case
tacctl sees the value that policy leaves.
.PP
tacctl also reads variables that move its files and set its clock for the test
suite
.RB ( TACCTL_ETC ,
.BR TACCTL_STATE_DIR ,
\&...). They are not an interface.
.SH FILES
.SS tacctl
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/
State directory, mode 0700, root only.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/store.yaml
The canonical users, groups, scopes and connection filters (shared secrets and
password hashes included). Mode 0600, root only. Print it with
.BR "tacctl store show" .
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/tacctl.yaml
Operator overrides of tacctl's tunables and settings (see
.BR "CONFIGURATION KEYS" ).
The defaults are built into tacctl and printed by
.BR "tacctl config defaults" ;
an empty file is deleted. A file that does not parse is never written into: a
setter refuses with
.RI "\(lqtacctl.yaml: could not parse " path ": " reason \(rq
and exits 1, and commands that only read use the defaults and warn once on
stderr.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/devices.yaml
The device registry (see
.BR "DEVICE REGISTRY" ):
registered device names with address, vendor, port, hostname,
description, legacy-ssh, pinned host keys and acknowledged notices, and the
registry's settings
.RB ( stale_days ,
.BR generic_names ).
Mode 0600, root only; absent means no devices. Edit it with
.BR "tacctl device" ,
which locks it, validates the result and replaces it atomically. Snapshots
include it. The pinned keys of enrolled hosts are kept under
.BR hosts: .
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/snmp.yaml
The SNMP community, or the v3 user and its passphrases, that
.B "device add"
and
.B "device check"
read a device's sysName with. Mode 0600, root only, written atomically by
.BR "tacctl config snmp" ;
never printed. Absent means none.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/snmp/\fIscope\fR.yaml
The SNMP community, or the v3 user and its passphrases, of one scope
.RB ( "tacctl scope snmp" ):
the credentials a device of that scope is read with, and printed in its
walkthroughs, instead of the default's. Mode 0600 in a 0700 directory, root
only; printed only by
.BR "scope snmp ... show \-\-reveal" .
Absent means the scope has none of its own. A scope's rename moves the file and
its removal deletes it.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/snmp/devices/\fIname\fR.yaml
The SNMP community, or the v3 user and its passphrases, of one registered
device
.RB ( "tacctl device snmp" ):
read in place of its scope's and the default's. The file is named for the
device in lower case. Mode 0600 in a 0700 directory, root only; printed only
by
.BR "device snmp ... show \-\-reveal" .
Absent means the device has none of its own. The file counts only while the
device has an
.B snmp:
map of its own; one without (such as a rollback to 0.2.3 leaves) is ignored,
not read, and said once by
.B "device show"
and
.BR "device snmp ... show" .
A device's rename moves the file, its removal and
.B "device import \-\-replace"
delete it, snapshots hold it, and
.B "backup restore"
makes the directory match the registry it restores. The device's non\-secret SNMP
settings are the
.B snmp:
map of its entry in
.IR devices.yaml .
.B "rollback 0.2.3"
removes that map and leaves the file.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/blocks
The directory of configuration blocks, 0700 root. A scope's blocks are under
its own name and a device's under
.IR _devices ;
a scope cannot be called
.BR _devices .
Snapshots include it and
.B backup restore
brings it back.
.B "rollback 0.2.3"
removes the lists in
.B tacctl.yaml
that put blocks in force and leaves the files.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/blocks/\fIscope\fR/\fIvendor\fR/\fIblock\fR
One configuration block of a scope: vendor syntax that tacctl owns beside the
structured settings.
.I vendor
is
.B juniper
or
.BR cisco ;
.I block
is
.B snmp
or
.BR system .
Mode 0600 in 0700 directories, root only; a block holds community strings, so
it is printed only by
.BR "scope block ... show \-\-reveal" .
A block is in force only while
.BR tacctl.yaml
names it
.RB ( blocks.\fIscope\fR.\fIvendor\fR ).
A scope's rename moves its blocks and its removal deletes them.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/blocks/_devices/\fIdevice\fR/\fIvendor\fR/\fIblock\fR
One configuration block of a registered device, in place of its scope's block
of the same object. The directory is named for the device in lower case; it is
named in
.B tacctl.yaml
by
.BR blocks_device.\fIdevice\fR.\fIvendor\fR ,
the device's dots written as
.BR ~ ,
and written by
.BR "device block" .
A device's rename moves it and its removal deletes it; a new device, or a
rename onto a name whose blocks are already on disk, is refused.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/console.yaml
The login console's switches per tier, per-user overrides and settings (see
.BR "CONFIGURATION KEYS" ).
Mode 0600, root only; absent means the defaults (the console on for every
tier). Edit it with
.BR "tacctl console" .
Snapshots include it.
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/tacctl\-console
The login console: a symbolic link to
.IR /usr/local/bin/tacctl ,
made by
.B install
and refreshed by
.BR upgrade .
.TP
.I /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/tacctl\-console.conf
sshd's drop-in for the members of
.B tac\-console
(the accounts whose shell is the console): the console forced, every
forwarding closed, key logins off. Written by
.B host sync
of the host enrolled with
.B \-\-local
and
.BR "console install" ,
checked with
.BR "sshd \-t" ,
refreshed by
.BR upgrade ,
removed by
.BR "console remove" ,
.B host unenroll
of that host and
.BR uninstall .
Do not edit.
.TP
.I /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/00\-tacctl\-engineer.conf
sshd's drop-in for the members of
.B tac\-engineer
(the engineer tier): every forwarding and tunnel closed whatever their shell
(agent forwarding follows
.BR "console agent\-forwarding" ).
The
.B 00\-
prefix makes sshd read it before
.IR tacctl\-console.conf ,
so an account in the engineer group and in a group the console lets forward (a
stale
.BR tac\-superuser )
stays locked down. Written at every
.B host sync
or
.B "host enroll \-\-local"
of the tacctl server and by
.BR "console install" ,
whether or not the console is installed; checked with
.BR "sshd \-t" ,
refreshed by
.BR upgrade ,
left in place by
.B "console remove"
and removed by
.BR uninstall .
Do not edit.
.TP
.I /etc/shells
Gets the line
.I /usr/local/bin/tacctl\-console
with the drop-in, and loses it with it.
.TP
.I /var/lib/tacctl/tier\-pinned
Marker that
.B "tacctl upgrade"
recorded the tier of every group at priv-lvl 15, once (a fresh install writes
it at once). It is outside
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
so a setting lost later is not recorded again by a routine upgrade.
.B uninstall
removes it.
.TP
.I /var/lib/tacctl/operator\-policy\-noted
Marker that
.B "tacctl upgrade"
printed, once, on its first run onto this release, the privilege lines a device
configured earlier keeps (a fresh
install writes it at once). Delete it to be told again.
.B uninstall
removes it.
.TP
.I /var/lib/tacctl/wti\-plain\-acl\-noted
Marker that
.B "tacctl upgrade"
named, once, on its first run onto this release, the scopes whose plain
management ACL entries permit every protocol
and port on WTI units (a fresh install writes it at once). Delete it to be told
again.
.B uninstall
removes it.
.TP
.I /var/lib/tacctl/ssh/known_hosts
Generated from
.I devices.yaml
on every registry write (and after
.BR "backup restore" ):
one
.I "name type key"
line per pinned host key, for ssh's
.B HostKeyAlias
lookup. Mode 0644, root-owned, in a directory 0755 under
.I /var/lib/tacctl
(0711: users pass through it, only root lists it), so every user's ssh can
read it. Do not edit it.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/rendered.json
The checksum of every file tacctl rendered, from which
.B status
and
.B config validate
report a hand edit as drift.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/.store.lock
.TQ
.I /etc/tacctl/.store\-apply.lock
Lock files, mode 0600, created when first needed and left in place. The first
serialises writes of the store; the second is the apply lock, taken by every
command that changes the store or
.I tacctl.yaml
and held through its restarts (see
.BR "STORE AND GENERATED CONFIGURATION" ).
Do not delete them while a command runs.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/<file>.overwritten\-<timestamp>
The version of
.IR store.yaml " or " tacctl.yaml
that a command found edited by hand while it ran and replaced when it put its
own copy back. Mode 0600; kept until removed.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/backups/
Automatic snapshots of the canonical files, one directory per snapshot, last
30 retained, root-only;
.I legacy/
holds old-style
.I tacquito.yaml.<timestamp>
backups, the pre-store config and files a render or
the state migration displaced.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/templates/
Host-specific overrides for the device config templates
.RI ( cisco ,
.IR cisco-legacy ,
.IR juniper ,
.IR wti ,
and for
.BR "--protocol radius"
.IR cisco-radius ,
.IR juniper-radius " and " wti-radius ).
A file named
.I <name>.template
here is used in place of the one shipped in
.IR config/templates/ .
Install puts a copy of each shipped template here, and
.B "tacctl upgrade"
replaces one with the new release's only while it is as tacctl wrote it:
.I .shipped.sha256
records what tacctl wrote (in
.BR sha256sum (1)
format). A changed template is kept, the new release's version is written
beside it as
.IR <name>.template.new ,
which is never used for rendering, and the upgrade warns, naming both. A
template with no record counts as unchanged when it is byte for byte a version
tacctl shipped, according to the git history of
.IR /opt/tacctl .
.TP
.IR /etc/tacctl/linux\-hosts ", " /etc/tacctl/linux\-uids
The registry of enrolled Linux hosts (with each host's method) and the
UID each user gets on every host.
.TP
.I /etc/tacctl/hosts/
One
.IR name .json
per enrolled host: what the last
.B host enroll
or
.B host sync
did (when, by whom, how it ended, the accounts it changed), the facts it read
of the host (operating system, sshd, PAM modules, the UID range of
.IR /etc/login.defs ),
and the last
.BR "host provisioner rotate" .
Printed by
.BR "host show" .
Mode 0600 in a 0700 directory, root only; renamed with the host and removed by
.BR "host unenroll" .
.TP
.I /var/lib/tacctl/linux/
The pam_tacplus source tarball and the modules built in containers.
.TP
.I /var/lib/tacctl/devices\-seen.json
The seen cache of
.BR "device scan" :
what the logs showed of each address, where each backend's next scan starts,
and the last host-key re-scan of each entry. Mode 0600, root only; derived
from the logs, not snapshotted, rebuilt by
.BR "device scan \-\-full" ;
.B uninstall
removes it.
.TP
.I /var/lib/tacctl/devices\-config.json
The record of each device's last configuration pull
.RB ( "device config pull" ):
when, by whom, over which transport, what the NETCONF probe found, the result
and the state of each managed section, keyed by the device's lower\-case name. Mode 0600, root only; derived from the devices, not
snapshotted, rebuilt by a pull. A rename carries a record along, a removal and
.B "device config forget"
delete it.
.B "rollback 0.2.3"
leaves it (as it leaves
.IR device\-config/ );
.B uninstall
removes it.
.TP
.I /var/lib/tacctl/device\-config/
One file per device,
.IR <name>.yaml :
the managed sections of its last successful pull, one literal block per
section, in the form they are compared in: a secret's value is not in it.
Mode 0600 in a 0700 directory, root only; derived, not snapshotted;
.B uninstall
removes the directory.
.TP
.I /var/lib/tacctl/device\-apply/
What
.B "device config apply"
keeps:
.I shapes.json
(0600: each shape's statements with every secret elided and who confirmed it)
and
.I locks/<name>.lock
(one lock file per device, so that one device is applied by one run at a
time). Root only; not snapshotted; no raw device output and no password is in
it.
.TP
.IR /etc/sudoers.d/tacctl ", " /etc/sudoers.d/tacctl\-tiers
Optional sudoers rules. Installed only via
.B "tacctl config sudoers install"
and
.BR "tacctl config sudoers tiers install" ;
.B "rollback 0.2.3"
rewrites a file that is exactly this release's text with 0.2.3's.
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/tacctl
The tacctl binary, built on the host from the clone in
.I /opt/tacctl
by
.B "tacctl install"
and
.BR "tacctl upgrade" .
.TP
.I /opt/tacctl/
Clone of the tacctl repository: the Go sources and vendored dependencies
tacctl is built from, the shipped templates, units, patches and this manual.
.B "tacctl upgrade"
pulls it.
.I /opt/tacctl/bin/tacctl.sh
is the bootstrap: it installs the Go toolchain if needed, builds the binary
when it is missing or out of date, and runs it.
.TP
.I /etc/bash_completion.d/tacctl
The bash completion script, generated by
.BR "tacctl completion bash" .
.TP
.I /usr/local/go/
The Go toolchain
.B install
fetches (the version the release pins) to build tacquito;
.I /usr/local/go/bin/go
is the compiler tacctl runs.
.TP
.I /etc/login.defs
Read, never written, by
.B "host enroll \-\-local"
for the UID range of this server's own accounts.
.TP
.I /etc/ssh/ssh_host_*_key.pub
The public host keys of a host, read over the login in use by
.BR "host enroll" ,
.B "host show \-\-check"
and
.B "host provisioner rotate"
to pin them and compare them with the registry.
.TP
.I /root/
Where
.B uninstall
leaves what it is asked to keep:
.IR tacquito\-backups\-<time>.tar.gz ,
.I tacquito\-logs\-<time>.tar.gz
(the accounting logs) and, for the RADIUS backend,
.IR tacctl\-radius\-logs\-<time>.tar.gz .
.TP
.I /tmp/
The default of
.B TMPDIR
(see
.BR ENVIRONMENT ):
where tacctl makes the private files and directories it stages while it works
and removes them afterwards.
.TP
.I /var/tmp/
Where
.B "scope block ... edit"
and
.B "device block ... edit"
make the private directory of the editor (mode 0700, handed to the invoking
user, removed afterwards on every path). It is not
.BR TMPDIR .
.TP
.I /usr/share/man/man1/tacctl.1.gz
This manual.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/tacctl/history
The
.B tacctl shell
history of the invoking user: mode 0600, the newest 1000 lines, redacted.
.SS TACACS+ (tacquito)
.TP
.I /etc/tacquito/tacquito.yaml
TACACS+ server configuration, rendered by tacctl from the store. Mode 0640,
owned by
.BR tacquito : tacquito .
The old paths of
.IR tacctl.yaml ,
.IR linux\-hosts ,
.IR linux\-uids ,
.I backups
and
.I templates
in
.I /etc/tacquito
lead into
.IR /etc/tacctl .
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/tacquito.service
Systemd unit file (the default listener).
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/tacquito@.service
Template unit, one instance per further listener.
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/tacquito.service.d/tacctl.conf
Systemd drop-in holding the listen address, log level, metrics address
and accounting log of the unit. Rendered by tacctl from
.I tacctl.yaml
.RB ( "tacctl config listen" ,
.BR "tacctl config loglevel" ,
.BR "tacctl config metrics" );
edits are overwritten. Instances have
.IR tacquito@<name>.service.d/tacctl.conf .
.TP
.I /var/log/tacquito/accounting.log
Per-session accounting records
.RI ( accounting\-<name>.log
per further listener). Rotated daily, retained 90 days
.RI ( /etc/logrotate.d/tacquito ).
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/tacquito
The tacquito server binary.
.TP
.I /usr/local/bin/tacquito-hashgen
Standalone bcrypt hash generator shipped with tacquito.
.TP
.I /opt/tacquito-src/
Clone of the tacquito server source code, used to rebuild the binary
during
.BR "tacctl upgrade" .
.SS RADIUS (FreeRADIUS)
.I <raddb>
is
.I /etc/freeradius/3.0
(Debian/Ubuntu) or
.I /etc/raddb
(RHEL family);
.I <logs>
is
.I /var/log/freeradius
or
.IR /var/log/radius .
No file of the FreeRADIUS package is edited.
.TP
.IR <raddb>/tacctl\-radius.conf ", " <raddb>/tacctl\-radius.users
tacctl's FreeRADIUS configuration (listeners, clients, policy) and its users
file (bcrypt hashes), rendered from the store; mode 0640, root and the
daemon's group.
.TP
.I <raddb>/tacctl\-radius\-dictionary/dictionary
The dictionary the daemon is started with: the package's, then the WTI
vendor and tacctl's internal attributes.
.TP
.IR /usr/sbin/freeradius ", " /usr/lib/freeradius/ ", " /usr/share/freeradius/dictionary ", " /run/freeradius/freeradius.pid
What the package provides and the configuration names: the daemon, its module
directory
.RI ( libdir ),
the main dictionary that
.I <raddb>/tacctl\-radius\-dictionary/dictionary
includes, and the pid file
.RI ( pidfile ).
On the RHEL family they are
.IR /usr/sbin/radiusd ,
.IR /usr/lib64/freeradius/ ,
the same dictionary and
.IR /run/radiusd/radiusd.pid .
Read, not edited.
.TP
.IR /etc/systemd/system/freeradius.service.d/tacctl.conf " (" radiusd.service.d " on RHEL)"
Drop-in that starts the distribution's unit as tacctl's instance; present
exactly while the backend is enabled.
.TP
.IR <logs>/tacctl\-auth.log ", " <logs>/tacctl\-accounting.log ", " <logs>/tacctl\-radius.log
One line per Access-Accept or Access-Reject; accounting detail records; the
daemon's log
.RI ( /etc/logrotate.d/tacctl\-radius ).
.SS Linux hosts
.TP
.IR /etc/pam.d/tacctl\-* ", " /etc/tacctl\-pam_radius.conf ", " /var/lib/tacctl\-client/
On an enrolled host: the PAM files tacctl writes (root-only; for tacplus
they hold the shared secret), the pam_radius_auth server file (0600), and
the client's state.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Install on a fresh host:
.B tacctl install
.TP
Add a superuser:
.B tacctl user add alice superuser
.TP
Restrict device subnets in the default scope:
.B tacctl scope prefixes lab add 10.1.0.0/24,172.16.5.0/24
.TP
Add a broad prod scope and a narrower lab scope (the /24 wins for 10.10.99.x):
.nf
.B "  tacctl scope add prod_dc_east --prefixes 10.10.0.0/16 --secret generate"
.B "  tacctl scope add lab_east     --prefixes 10.10.99.0/24 --secret generate"
.fi
.TP
Trace which scope a client IP falls into:
.B tacctl scope lookup 10.10.99.7
.TP
Grant a user full production access across two datacenters:
.B tacctl user scope alice add prod_dc_east,prod_dc_west
.TP
Generate a Cisco-ready configuration for the prod scope:
.B tacctl config cisco --scope prod_dc_east
.TP
Generate legacy IOS 12.x syntax for an older device:
.B tacctl config cisco --scope prod_dc_east --legacy
.TP
Print the serial-menu walkthrough for a WTI console server in the lab scope:
.B tacctl config wti --scope lab_east
.TP
Serve the same users over RADIUS, with Cisco and Juniper privilege attributes for prod:
.nf
.B "  tacctl backend enable radius"
.B "  tacctl scope vendor-attrs prod_dc_east enable cisco,juniper"
.B "  tacctl config cisco --scope prod_dc_east --protocol radius"
.fi
.TP
Tag one address of a scope as a WTI unit (it gets WTI-Super and nothing else):
.B tacctl scope devices lab_east set 10.10.99.5 wti
.TP
Enroll a Linux host with pam_radius_auth, then switch it to pam_tacplus:
.nf
.B "  tacctl host enroll admin@web1 --method radius"
.B "  tacctl host enroll admin@web1 --method tacplus"
.fi
.TP
Bind the listeners to a specific management IP:
.nf
.B "  tacctl config listen tcp 10.1.0.1:49"
.B "  tacctl config listen --backend radius --listener auth udp 10.1.0.1:1812"
.fi
.TP
Check, without writing anything, whether a tacquito.yaml can move into the store:
.B tacctl store import --check
.TP
Expose the Prometheus exporter to an external scraper (default is loopback-only):
.B tacctl config metrics address 10.1.0.1:8080
.TP
Enable passwordless invocation for members of the adm group:
.B tacctl config sudoers install adm
.TP
Print client-side bcrypt generation instructions to share with a user:
.B tacctl hash commands
.SH NOTES
.B IPv4 only by default.
The TACACS+ listener runs with
.B -network tcp
and the RADIUS ones with
.BR udp ,
which bind IPv4 only. Switching to
.B tcp6
or
.B udp6
enables a dual-stack socket on most platforms; IPv4 clients then
connect with IPv4-mapped addresses
.RB ( ::ffff:10.1.0.1 )
that do not match IPv4 prefix ACLs configured via
.BR "tacctl scope prefixes" ,
.BR "config allow" ,
or
.BR "config deny" .
Add equivalent IPv6 prefix rules before enabling
.B tcp6.
.PP
.B Juniper template users are required.
Every
.B local-user-name
referenced by a group class (for example
.BR RO-CLASS ,
.BR OP-CLASS ,
.BR RW-CLASS )
must have a matching local user on each Juniper device. Without it,
authentication succeeds but Junos rejects the login silently.
Run
.B tacctl config juniper
to regenerate the device-side configuration after adding or editing
groups.
.PP
.B Built on the host.
tacctl is a single Go binary, built on the server from the sources in the
clone
.I /opt/tacctl
(its dependencies are vendored there: no network or module proxy is used for
the build) with the Go toolchain in
.IR /usr/local/go ,
which
.I /opt/tacctl/bin/tacctl.sh
installs when it is missing, after checking the published SHA-256 of the
download. The first build fills about 130 MB of Go build cache; later builds
reuse the build cache of root
.RI ( /root/.cache/go-build ).
.B "tacctl version \-\-long"
prints the commit and build date of the installed binary, which equals the
commit of the clone after every
.BR "tacctl install" " or " "tacctl upgrade" .
